window.EXPL = {"2014A-p10#a": "<p>The simplex algorithm does not work directly with inequalities: it manipulates a system of linear <em>equalities</em> together with the requirement that every variable be non-negative. So the whole job here is to turn each of the two inequality constraints into an equality by introducing one new non-negative variable per constraint, without changing the objective.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>The first constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> has slack on its left side: the left-hand side may fall short of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. We absorb that gap with a non-negative <em>slack</em> variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, giving the equality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Whenever <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> held, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the two forms are equivalent.</li>\n  <li>The second constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math> points the other way, so the left side <em>exceeds</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>14</mn></mrow></math>. We remove that surplus by subtracting a non-negative variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math>. Again <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> reproduces the original inequality.</li>\n  <li>The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> is left untouched (the new variables have coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in it), and we keep the sign restrictions on all variables.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mrow><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>This is exactly the standard form the simplex method needs: two equalities in five non-negative variables, ready to be read off as a basis (here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>) with the remaining variables set to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#b": "<p>A basis picks two of the five variables to be <em>basic</em>; the other three are <em>non-basic</em> and forced to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Here the basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so the non-basic variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The basic-feasible solution (the vertex) is obtained by solving the two equality constraints for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> once the non-basic variables are zeroed out.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Solve the first equality for the basic variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>: from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Solve the second equality for the basic variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>: from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn></mrow></math>. To express <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> purely in terms of non-basic variables, substitute the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> from step 1:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>14</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>Now set every non-basic variable to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). Step 1 gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and step 2 gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Both basic values came out non-negative (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>), so this basis is <em>feasible</em> and the point is a genuine vertex of the feasible polytope:</p>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>The rewritten equations <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> are exactly the \"dictionary\" the simplex algorithm will read in the next parts to decide how to pivot.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#c": "<p>To decide which variable should enter the basis, simplex rewrites the objective so that it only mentions the current non-basic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> (which are all at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). The sign of each coefficient then tells us whether nudging that variable up from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> would raise or lower the objective.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Take the objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> and replace the basic variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> by its dictionary expression <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> from part (b):\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>Expand and collect like terms:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>Read off the coefficients of the non-basic variables: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> have positive coefficients, increasing either of them (from its current value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) increases the objective; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> would decrease it, so it is not a candidate.</li>\n  <li>Among the improving variables we pick the one with the largest coefficient, i.e. the steepest per-unit gain. That is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> with rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> versus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\nThe entering variable is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>: it offers the best rate of increase of the objective, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> per unit.\n</div>\n<p>This is the standard (Dantzig) pivoting rule — enter the non-basic variable with the most positive reduced cost — and it sets up the ratio test in part (d) that determines who must leave.</p>\n", "2014A-p10#d": "<p>We are raising the entering variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, while the other non-basic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> stay at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The leaving variable is the first currently-basic variable that would be driven down to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> grows. We find it by looking at how each basic variable depends on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> in the dictionary from part (b).</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Write the basic variables with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>Check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>: its expression contains no <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> term, so increasing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> does not change <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>; it stays at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and never forces us to stop. It imposes no bound.</li>\n  <li>Check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>: it equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which <em>decreases</em> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> increases and reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. The non-negativity requirement <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> therefore caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. This is the min-ratio test: the only limiting basic variable has ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>The binding variable, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, is the one pushed to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves the basis and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> takes its place at value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>After the pivot we solve the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-equation for the new basic variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>: from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> is unchanged.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">\nThe variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves the basis; the new basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.\n</div>\n", "2014A-p10#e": "<p>The objective value at any basic solution is found by plugging the current variable values into the objective. The most convenient form is the one from part (c), where the objective is already written using only the non-basic variables plus a constant.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Recall from part (c) that the objective equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Identify the new solution after the pivot of parts (c)–(d): the basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so the non-basic variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, while the entering variable reached <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Substitute these values:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>32</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\nAfter the first pivot the objective value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>32</mn></mrow></math>.\n</div>\n<p>As a sanity check, this matches the increase predicted by the pivot: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> entered with per-unit rate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and rose by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn></mrow></math> to the previous objective value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2014A-p4#a": "<p>We model \"choose a minimum-weight set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> that dominates every vertex\". Introduce one binary variable per vertex: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> assignment is then the same as a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Objective.</strong> The weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> (terms with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> drop out). We minimize it: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Domination constraint.</strong> A vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is dominated iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> or some neighbor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>; equivalently, at least one vertex of the closed neighborhood <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is chosen. In LP terms: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mrow></math>, for <em>every</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> values this sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when at least one of those vertices has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Integrality.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> makes it an integer program (and encodes the yes/no membership).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the ILP is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector is a dominating set of that weight, and conversely, so optimal ILP value = minimum weight dominating set.</p>\n", "2014A-p4#b": "<p>We need an instance where the LP relaxation (same constraints, but <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>) has optimum strictly below the integer optimum. The example is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>-cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> with all weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; the fractional solution sets three vertices to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and the fourth to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: fractional <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> can \"spread\" domination across a vertex's closed neighborhood, satisfying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> more cheaply than any integral choice can.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Setup.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> has vertices on a square; each vertex has exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> neighbors, so its closed neighborhood <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices. Assign one vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and the other three <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Feasibility.</strong> Check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. The zero vertex: its two neighbors are the two adjacent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> vertices, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> vertex: its closed neighborhood always contains at least two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> vertices, so its sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. All constraints hold.</li>\n<li><strong>LP cost.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. So the LP optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Integer cost.</strong> One chosen vertex dominates only itself plus its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> neighbors = <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> vertices, missing the opposite corner, so a single vertex is not enough; a minimum dominating set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertices, cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence LP optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> ILP optimum: the relaxation is not integral (an integrality gap of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>).</p>\n", "2014A-p5#x": "<p>We want a minimum-cost subgraph in which every receiver <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> has a path to <em>some</em> sender in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> (senders may differ across receivers). The trick is to merge all senders into a single artificial terminal so the problem becomes an ordinary Steiner tree, for which we already have a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Reduction.</strong> Add one new artificial vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> and connect <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to every sender <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> with an edge of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Call this augmented graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Equivalence of the two problems.</strong> In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, a receiver <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> is connected to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> if and only if it is connected (in the original graph) to some sender: a path from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> must use one of the zero-cost edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so it reaches a sender <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>; conversely any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>-to-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> path extends to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> for free. Requiring every receiver to reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is exactly requiring every receiver to reach some sender.</li>\n<li><strong>Steiner-tree formulation.</strong> A minimum-cost subgraph of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> connecting all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is a minimum-cost Steiner tree with required (terminal) set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Because the added edges cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the optimal Steiner-tree cost in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> equals the optimal cost of the original connection problem.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply the known algorithm.</strong> Run the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for Steiner tree (proved in Problem Set 2) on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> with terminals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. It returns a subgraph of cost at most twice the optimal Steiner-tree cost, hence at most twice the optimum of our problem. Dropping the zero-cost artificial edges yields the desired subgraph of the original graph, giving a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p6#x": "<p>This is Karatsuba's algorithm: multiply two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-bit integers with only <em>three</em> half-size multiplications instead of the naive four, giving the recurrence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: the middle coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> is recovered from the single product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by subtracting the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> we already compute — trading one multiplication for a few cheap additions.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Split.</strong> Write each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-bit number by its high and low halves: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-bit numbers. (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> = top <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> bits, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> = bottom <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> bits; the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> factor is a shift.)</li>\n<li><strong>Expand the product.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math> Naively this needs the four products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Karatsuba's trick.</strong> Note <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, so the needed middle term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, and we only compute the three products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, each on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-bit numbers (the sums <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bits — still <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li><strong>Cost of the glue.</strong> Everything else is a constant number of additions/subtractions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-bit numbers (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> each by the allowed basic op) and shifts by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> each). So the non-recursive work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Solve.</strong> Master Theorem with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1.585</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, beating the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> schoolbook method.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p8#a": "<p>MAX-3SAT input: a 3CNF formula with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> clauses over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> variables, each clause an OR of three literals from distinct variables. The randomized algorithm is as simple as possible, and linearity of expectation does the work.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: a random assignment satisfies each individual clause with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>; summing these per-clause probabilities gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> satisfied clauses in expectation, no independence needed.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Algorithm.</strong> Set each variable to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> independently and uniformly at random (a fair coin per variable).</li>\n<li><strong>Probability one clause is satisfied.</strong> A clause of 3 literals is <em>un</em>satisfied only when all three literals are false. The three literals come from distinct variables, so their truth values are independent; each literal is false with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>clause&#x000A0;unsatisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>clause&#x000A0;satisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Indicators.</strong> For clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is satisfied. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. The total number satisfied is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Linearity of expectation.</strong> Regardless of any dependence between clauses (they may share variables),\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Approximation ratio.</strong> The optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> can satisfy at most every clause, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, making this a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>-approximation in expectation.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014A-p8#b": "<p>We bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>fewer&#x000A0;than&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;of&#x000A0;clauses&#x000A0;satisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> in closed form. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the number of satisfied clauses; from part (a), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: \"few satisfied\" means \"many unsatisfied.\" Switch to the count of <em>unsatisfied</em> clauses, which is nonnegative, so Markov's inequality applies directly.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Rewrite the event.</strong> Satisfying fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> clauses means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. The number of unsatisfied clauses is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Expectation of the unsatisfied count.</strong> By linearity, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Markov's inequality.</strong> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, Markov gives, for the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>m</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, a closed-form bound.</p>\n<p><strong>Why not Chernoff:</strong> a Chernoff bound would need the summands <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> to be independent, but clauses can share variables, so the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are generally dependent. The solution notes that <em>if</em> we (incorrectly) assumed independence, Chernoff on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo></mrow></math> would give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>8</mn><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>168</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> — much sharper, but not rigorously justified here. Markov needs no independence, which is why it is the correct tool.</p>\n", "2014A-p9#a": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be any maximal matching (no edge can be added to it while keeping it a matching) and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> be a maximum matching (largest possible). We must show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> is never worse than half the optimum — a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Every edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is blocked by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Take any edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. At least one of its two endpoints must already be an endpoint of some edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. Otherwise both endpoints of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> would be untouched by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> shares no vertex with any edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> would be a matching strictly larger than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> — contradicting maximality of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Charge each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>-edge to a matched vertex.</strong> Map each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> to one of its endpoints that lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the set of vertices touched by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> edges, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>This map is injective.</strong> Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is a matching, its edges are pairwise vertex-disjoint, so no vertex is an endpoint of two different edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. Hence distinct edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> map to distinct vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Count.</strong> An injection from the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Combined with the trivial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> (since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> is maximum), the size of any maximal matching lies within a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> of the maximum, which is the claimed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n", "2014B-p7#a": "<p>Bin packing input: items <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; pack all items into as few bins as possible, each bin holding total weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. We model it as an ILP.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Bound the number of bins.</strong> There is always a valid packing using at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bins — put each item in its own bin (each holds one item of weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, assuming weights are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>). So we may index candidate bins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and never need more.</li>\n<li><strong>Variables.</strong> Two families of 0/1 variables. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is placed in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is \"open\" (used at all). Both are forced into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Objective.</strong> Minimize the number of open bins, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Each item packed.</strong> Every item must land in at least one bin: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. (At the optimum this is exactly one bin.)</li>\n<li><strong>Only open bins receive items.</strong> If item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> then bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> must be open: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. This links the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> variables so the objective actually counts every bin we use.</li>\n<li><strong>Capacity.</strong> Each bin's total weight is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Full ILP:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;s.t.&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n", "2014B-p7#b": "<p>Now the number of bins is fixed (given as input, say <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> machines), all bins share one common capacity, and we want the <em>smallest</em> capacity that lets all items fit. This is exactly the makespan / load-balancing problem: minimize the maximum load over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> bins.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: the minimum feasible capacity equals the minimum achievable maximum load. So run the greedy least-loaded scheduler and report the max load it produces.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Reformulation.</strong> If every bin has capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, all items fit iff we can assign items to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> bins with every bin's total load <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. The least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> for which this is possible is precisely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>min</mo><mrow><mtext>assignments</mtext></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>max&#x000A0;bin&#x000A0;load</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So minimizing capacity = minimizing makespan.</li>\n<li><strong>Algorithm (greedy list scheduling).</strong> Process items one by one; place each item into the currently <em>least-loaded</em> bin. Output the maximum bin load of the resulting packing as the capacity.</li>\n<li><strong>Why 2-approximation.</strong> This is the scheduling result proved in pset 2 (problem 3): greedy least-loaded assignment yields a maximum load at most twice the optimal makespan. Two standard lower bounds drive it — the optimal makespan is at least the average load <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and at least the largest single item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. When the greedy max-load bin last received an item, that bin was the least loaded, so its load-before-adding was <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> average <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>; adding one more item (size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>) keeps it <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Conclusion.</strong> The reported capacity (= greedy's max load) is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> times the minimum feasible capacity, giving a 2-approximation, computable in polynomial time.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2014B-p8#a": "<p>The integer program IP1 is the <strong>minimum vertex cover</strong> problem. To see this, read off what each ingredient of the program means on the graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>The variables.</strong> There is one binary variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> per vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. Interpret <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> as \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is selected into the set\" and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> as \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is left out.\" So a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> assignment is exactly a choice of a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The constraints.</strong> For each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> we require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Since the variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, this holds iff at least one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is selected. In words: every edge has at least one endpoint in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> — precisely the definition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> being a <em>vertex cover</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The objective.</strong> Minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> minimizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, the number of selected vertices.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Choosing a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> point that covers every edge while minimizing the count of chosen vertices is exactly asking for a smallest vertex cover. Hence IP1 computes the minimum vertex cover of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</div>\n", "2014B-p8#b": "<p>The LP relaxation LP1 is obtained from IP1 by dropping the integrality requirement: replace each constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with the continuous constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The relaxed program is</p>\n<p><div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Why only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</strong> A relaxation must enlarge the feasible region. The box <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> becomes the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, which is described by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. We keep the lower bound but can safely omit the upper bound.</li>\n  <li><strong>Why <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is redundant.</strong> The objective is a minimization and every constraint on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a lower bound (edge constraints and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). If some optimal solution had <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, lowering that coordinate down to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> keeps all edge constraints satisfied — an endpoint value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> already satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — and strictly decreases the objective. So no optimal solution ever needs a coordinate above <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; imposing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> does not change the optimum.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Thus LP1 with just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has the same optimal value as the version with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which is why the upper bound is called redundant.</div>\n", "2014B-p8#c": "<p>We dualize LP1, the primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The dual has one variable per primal constraint and one constraint per primal variable.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Dual variables.</strong> Each primal inequality is an edge constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so we introduce one dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> per edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dual objective.</strong> The right-hand sides of the primal constraints are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and the dual maximizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Dual constraints.</strong> There is one dual constraint per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. In the primal, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> appears (with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) exactly in the constraints of edges incident to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Its primal objective coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, giving the dual constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sign.</strong> Because the primal variables satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the dual constraints are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>; and dualizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> primal constraints in a min-problem yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The dual DLP1 is <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> It puts a weight on each edge so that the weights around any vertex sum to at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, maximizing total weight — a fractional matching.</div>\n", "2014B-p8#d": "<p>We now read the integer version of DLP1 — the dual — as a graph problem. Adding integrality means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for every edge, so an assignment picks a subset of edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>The variables.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> indicates whether edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is selected. The point is thus a choice of an edge subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The constraints.</strong> For each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says that among the edges incident to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, at most one is selected. Equivalently, no two selected edges share an endpoint — exactly the definition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> being a <em>matching</em>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The objective.</strong> Maximizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> maximizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, the number of selected edges.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">So the integer program corresponding to DLP1 asks for the largest set of pairwise non-adjacent edges: this is the <strong>maximum matching</strong> problem. It is the natural dual counterpart to the minimum vertex cover of part (a) — every edge in a matching must be covered by a distinct vertex, so matching size lower-bounds cover size.</div>\n", "2015A-p5#x": "<p>We sample points uniformly in the unit cube and count how many land inside the radius-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> ball, whose volume is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th sample falls inside the ball; since the cube has volume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>:</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, and the algorithm outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, whose expectation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Translate the accuracy requirement into a deviation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> We want <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>6</mn><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Dividing the inequality inside by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>; multiplying by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> gives a deviation of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> from its mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>. So we need the relative deviation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply the (two-sided) Chernoff bound</strong> for a sum of independent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> variables, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B4;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>6</mn><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>exp</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>exp</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Force this below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</strong> Require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1800</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Taking logs: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1800</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, which rearranges to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>1800</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>:</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1800</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">A number of samples <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1800</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>8</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x003C0;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> suffices (a closed-form expression, as requested).</div>", "2015A-p6#a": "<p>Every item weight is a unit fraction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>; the algorithm processes items in decreasing weight and puts each into any bin that still fits it, opening a new bin only when none does. We prove by induction on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> that after all items of weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> are packed, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bins are not full.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Base case <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</strong> Items of weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> are those of weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (each fills a bin exactly) and weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> (which pack two per bin). Two halves fill a bin completely, so the only possible non-full bin is one holding a single unpaired <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> item. Hence at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> non-full bin.</li>\n  <li><strong>Inductive hypothesis.</strong> Assume that after packing all items of weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> non-full bins.</li>\n  <li><strong>Packing the weight-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> items.</strong> These items are now placed. Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the algorithm can keep dropping <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> items into a bin until it is filled to exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; it never has to open a new bin while any bin it is currently filling still has room. So among the bins opened during this phase, every one except possibly the last-opened ends up completely full.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The number of non-full bins is therefore at most the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> from before plus at most one newly opened non-full bin, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> non-full bins after packing all items of weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. This completes the induction.</div>\n", "2015A-p6#b": "<p>Suppose that during the run the very last item that forced a new bin to be opened had weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. We give a lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> on the load of every bin other than the one opened last.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>What \"opening a new bin\" tells us.</strong> The algorithm opens a new bin for an item only when the item does not fit in any existing bin. So at the moment this weight-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> item was placed, every already-open bin had less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> of free space — otherwise the item would have gone there instead.</li>\n  <li><strong>Translate free space into load.</strong> Free space below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> means each of those bins was filled to more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> of every such bin was already occupied.</li>\n  <li><strong>Loads only grow afterwards.</strong> Since this was the <em>last</em> bin-opening event, any items placed after it are merely added into existing bins, so no bin's load ever decreases. Every bin except the last-opened therefore keeps a load of at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> at the end.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Hence in the final packing, each bin other than the one opened last contains items of total weight at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</div>\n", "2015A-p6#c": "<p>We combine parts (a) and (b) to show the algorithm uses at most one bin more than the optimum. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> be the number of full bins and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> the number of non-full bins it produces, so the algorithm uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> bins. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the weight of the item that opened the last bin.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Lower-bound the total weight.</strong> Each full bin holds weight exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, contributing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. By part (b), each non-full bin except the last-opened one holds at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> such bins. So the total item weight is at least <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>k</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><strong>Use part (a).</strong> Part (a) gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Plugging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> into the bound, the total weight is strictly greater than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Compare with OPT.</strong> Every bin holds weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so any valid packing needs at least (total weight) bins; in particular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>total&#x000A0;weight</mtext><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Since OPT is an integer, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The algorithm uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> bins while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so it uses at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> bins. This is an additive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</div>\n", "2015A-p7#a": "<p>We turn the three \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" inequalities into equalities by adding a non-negative slack variable to each, then run one simplex pivot in dictionary form (each basic variable and the objective written in terms of the current non-basic variables).</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Initial dictionary.</strong> With slacks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the non-basic variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> (all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>):\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Entering variable.</strong> Choose a non-basic variable with a positive objective coefficient; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> has the largest (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> enters.</li>\n<li><strong>Ratio test (leaving variable).</strong> Increase <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and see which basic hits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> first. In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn></mrow></math>; in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> only grows (no bound); in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. The minimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves.</li>\n<li><strong>Solve for the entering variable.</strong> From the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> row, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Substitute</strong> this into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>40</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>6</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>9</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>37</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>11</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>: substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>, which adds to the original <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> to give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>. (The printed solution writes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math> here, a small slip; the correct coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math>.)</p>\n", "2015A-p7#b": "<p>We form the dual by the standard recipe for a primal in the form <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Each primal constraint gives a dual variable, and each primal variable gives a dual constraint.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>There are three primal constraints, so introduce dual variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (one per constraint). Their right-hand sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>12</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> become the dual objective, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>12</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Each primal variable produces one dual constraint of the form \"(its column of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>)<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>y</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> (its objective coefficient)\". Read the coefficients column by column from the primal.</li>\n<li><strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math></strong> (column <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>; objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math></strong> (column <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math></strong> (column <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>; objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></mrow></math></strong> (column <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Dual LP: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>12</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The inequalities flip to \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" and constraints become variables (and vice versa).</div>\n", "2015A-p7#c": "<p>We want a lower bound of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math> on the dual optimum. We get it for free by exhibiting one feasible primal solution of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math> and then invoking LP duality — no dual computation needed.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Part (a) already produced such a solution. After the pivot, set the non-basic variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>37</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Check feasibility: all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and all original variables are non-negative, so every constraint of the primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></math>-LP is satisfied. The objective value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>4</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Because the primal is a maximization and we have a feasible point of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>, the primal optimum is <em>at least</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math> (any feasible value is a lower bound on the max).</li>\n<li>By the strong duality theorem, the dual optimum equals the primal optimum. Therefore the dual optimum is also at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The logic chain: feasible primal value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>40</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>primal&#x000A0;optimum</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>dual&#x000A0;optimum</mtext></mrow></math>. Hence the dual optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>40</mn></mrow></math>. We only needed one concrete feasible primal solution plus strong duality.</div>\n", "2015A-p7#d": "<p>The obstacle is that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is not linear. The standard fix is to replace each absolute value by a fresh variable that is forced to equal it at the optimum, using the identity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;and&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> to stand for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> to stand for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The objective becomes the linear <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For each absolute value, add the two \"sandwich\" constraints. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the smallest number that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li>Why this is exact: each pair of constraints forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. Because we are <em>minimizing</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, the optimum pushes each variable down to its lower bound, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. So the LP optimum equals the original objective.</li>\n<li>Sign restrictions: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (automatically true since they dominate a value and its negation, but stated explicitly), while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> remain free (unrestricted) variables.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Resulting LP: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</div>\n", "2015B-p3#x": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> be the weight of a minimum TSP tour and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mrow></math> the weight of a minimum-weight perfect (maximum) matching in the complete weighted graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. We prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> by extracting two matchings from an optimal tour.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Setup.</strong> Assume without loss of generality that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is even (a perfect matching needs an even number of vertices). Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the vertices in the order they are visited by a minimum TSP tour; the tour uses the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, of total weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Split the tour's edges into two matchings.</strong> Take the alternating edges\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nEach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> touches every vertex exactly once, so both are perfect (hence maximum) matchings, and together they use each tour edge exactly once, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>One of them is cheap.</strong> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, the smaller of the two satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> (the minimum is at most the average).</li>\n<li><strong>Conclude.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is the minimum weight over <em>all</em> perfect matchings, so it is at most the weight of either <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>; in particular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">C</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, as required.</p>", "2015B-p5#x": "<p>We are given a Las Vegas algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>: it is <em>always correct</em>, but its running time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is random with expectation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. We must build a Monte Carlo algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> that always runs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time and is correct with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. The idea is to run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> but cut it off after a fixed budget of steps, using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> as a black box.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> finishes within that budget, output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>'s answer. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> has not finished, halt it and output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Running time.</strong> By construction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> never runs more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps (plus the trivial cutoff overhead), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> runs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time on every execution — deterministically bounded.</li>\n<li><strong>When can <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> err?</strong> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> finishes, its answer is correct (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is Las Vegas, always correct), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is correct. The only way <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> can be wrong is when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> fails to finish within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> outputs the default <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bounding that probability.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so Markov's inequality applies:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> errs with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. it is correct with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. This bound uses only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so it depends solely on the algorithm's internal randomness and not on the particular input <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, exactly as required.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#a": "<p>Read the ILP variable by variable. There is one binary variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> per vertex, which we read as: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means \"put <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> in the chosen set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>\", <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means \"leave it out\". A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector is therefore the same thing as a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> counts how many vertices are chosen, so maximizing it means picking a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> of maximum size.</li>\n<li>The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> says that among the two endpoints of any edge, their indicators sum to at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> values that forbids <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: the two endpoints of an edge cannot both be chosen.</li>\n<li>\"No edge has both endpoints in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>\" is exactly the definition of an independent set: no two chosen vertices are adjacent.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So we are choosing a largest vertex subset with no edge inside it. This is the <em>maximum independent set</em> problem.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#b": "<p>An <em>LP relaxation</em> keeps the same objective and the same linear constraints of the ILP but drops the integrality requirement, replacing the discrete set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The variables may now take fractional values, which is what makes the program a linear program (solvable in polynomial time).</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Objective and edge constraints are copied unchanged: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>The upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is redundant here, so it is dropped: for any edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> already forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. (The graph is connected, so every vertex lies on some edge.) Hence writing only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> gives the same feasible region.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is why the official relaxation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#c": "<p>To lower-bound a maximization LP it suffices to exhibit <em>one</em> feasible point: the optimum is at least the objective value of any feasible solution. The proof does exactly this with the \"all one-halves\" point.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: set every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. It satisfies every constraint with room to spare, and its objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so the LP optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Feasibility of the edge constraints: for any edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. So each edge constraint holds (with equality).</li>\n<li>Nonnegativity: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Hence the point is feasible.</li>\n<li>Objective value: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Because the LP maximizes, its optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> the value of this feasible point, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>L</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is notable because the integer optimum (maximum independent set) can be as small as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, e.g. on a clique — so the relaxation can be a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> larger than the true integer answer.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#d": "<p>We want a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>-vertex graph on which the maximum independent set has size exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Take the <em>clique</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>: the complete graph where every pair of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></math> vertices is joined by an edge.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Any two distinct vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> are adjacent (that is what \"complete\" means). So no set containing two vertices can be independent — every such pair is an edge, violating independence.</li>\n<li>Therefore every independent set has at most one vertex.</li>\n<li>A single vertex is trivially independent (there is no pair inside it to form an edge), so size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is achievable.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence the ILP optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Combined with part (c), on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>K</mi><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> the LP optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>50</mn></mrow></math> while the ILP optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: a large integrality gap.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#e": "<p>The dual is built mechanically from the primal (b): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. For a maximization LP with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> constraints and nonnegative variables, the rules are: one dual variable per primal constraint, the dual minimizes, primal right-hand sides become the dual objective, primal objective coefficients become dual right-hand sides, and the constraint matrix is transposed. The dual has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> constraints and nonnegative dual variables.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>One dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> per primal constraint, i.e. per edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Dual objective = primal RHS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Each edge constraint has RHS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>One dual constraint per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. In the transposed matrix, the column of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> lists the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> appears in: exactly the edges incident to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, each with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The RHS of this dual constraint is the primal objective coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Hence for every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mstyle></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> — the sum over edges touching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2015B-p7#f": "<p>Read the dual of (e) as an ILP by forcing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means \"select edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" and a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector is a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>F</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> of edges.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> says: at least one selected edge is incident to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. In words, every vertex is <em>covered</em> (touched) by some chosen edge.</li>\n<li>The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts the selected edges, so we want as few edges as possible.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Choosing a smallest set of edges so that every vertex is incident to at least one of them is exactly the <em>minimum edge cover</em> problem. (It is the natural \"dual\" companion to the maximum independent set problem we started from.)</p>\n", "2015B-p7#g": "<p>Goal: compute a minimum edge cover in polynomial time. (An edge cover exists only if there are no isolated vertices; here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is connected with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so every vertex has an edge.)</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: a maximum matching covers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math> vertices with as few edges as possible; then spend exactly one extra edge per still-uncovered vertex.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Algorithm.</strong> Compute a maximum matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> (polynomial time, e.g. Blossom / the matching algorithm from class). <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math> edges and covers <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math> distinct vertices. For each of the remaining <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math> unmatched vertices, add one arbitrary edge incident to it. The result covers all vertices.</li>\n<li><strong>Size of the output.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></li>\n<li><strong>Optimality (lower bound on any cover).</strong> Take any optimal cover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Assign each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to one edge of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> that covers it. Every vertex gets an edge, and each edge is assigned to at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertices (its two endpoints), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> number of edges that received an assignment.</li>\n<li>The edges assigned to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertices are vertex-disjoint on those assignments, hence form a matching; a matching has at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math> edges. So at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math> assigned edges cover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> vertices and the rest cover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Counting vertices: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>double&#x000A0;edges</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>single&#x000A0;edges</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the number of assigned edges is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mtext>MAX-MATCHING</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is optimal we also have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>: the algorithm is exactly optimal.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2016A-p5#x": "<p>The claim \"the greedy algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for maximum cut\" is <strong>false</strong>; we refute it by tracing the algorithm and showing it can output a cut of value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which no finite approximation ratio can justify.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Read the rule carefully.</strong> Both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> start empty. A vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> is placed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> only \"if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> has more neighbors at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> than at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>\"; otherwise it goes to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. Note the tie case (equal counts) fails the strict \"more than\", so it defaults to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>First vertex.</strong> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> both empty, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> neighbors in each. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is false, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is still empty.</li>\n<li><strong>By induction, everything goes to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> As long as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> stays empty, every later vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> neighbors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> neighbors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>; the condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>neighbors&#x000A0;in&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is never true, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> again. Hence <em>all</em> vertices end in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>The cut is empty.</strong> The cut counts edges with one endpoint in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and one in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>; with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> that count is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Yet for any graph with at least one edge the optimum cut is positive (e.g. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>).</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the algorithm outputs value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. A <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>-approximation would require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which is violated. The ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow></mrow></math> is unbounded (and the output is not even a valid partition into two non-empty sets), so the claim is refuted.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#a": "<p>A matching is a set of edges no two of which share a vertex. We encode the decision \"is edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> in the matching?\" with a binary variable and translate the two defining properties of a matching into an objective and a family of constraints.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> that is forced to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; the last line <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> makes this an <em>integer</em> (binary) program. Reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> as \"edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is selected\" and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> as \"not selected\".</li>\n  <li>The total weight of the selected edges is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; since we want a maximum-weight matching we maximize this sum.</li>\n  <li>The matching property is exactly: no vertex is used by two selected edges. For a fixed vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the edges touching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> are those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, and the count of selected ones is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> (sum over edges incident to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>). Requiring at most one selected edge at each vertex gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Putting it together yields the binary integer program</p>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>Any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector satisfies \"each vertex covered at most once\", i.e. it is the indicator vector of a matching, and its objective value is that matching's weight, so an optimal solution is a maximum-weight matching.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#b": "<p>We dualize the LP relaxation of part (a), i.e. the program <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (the integrality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is dropped for the relaxation).</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>The primal is a maximization with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and nonnegative variables. The standard dual is then a minimization with \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraints and nonnegative dual variables.</li>\n  <li>There is one dual variable per primal constraint. The primal has one constraint per vertex, so we get a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>There is one dual constraint per primal variable, i.e. per edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. The column of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in the constraint matrix has a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly in the two rows for its endpoints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> (edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> appears in vertex-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>'s and vertex-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>'s constraints). Hence the dual constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where the right-hand side is the primal objective coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>The dual objective coefficients are the primal right-hand sides, all equal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; so we minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>By weak duality every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> upper-bounds the matching LP value, which is the intuition behind the covering interpretation in part (c).</p>\n", "2016A-p6#c": "<p>We read off what the dual LP of part (b) is \"asking for\". Recall it is</p>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Interpret each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> as an amount of a \"resource\" (a weight, or budget) that we place on vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> says: for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the total resource sitting on its two endpoints must be at least the edge's demand <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So every edge is \"covered\" by the resource at its endpoints.</li>\n  <li>The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> says we want to use as little total resource as possible.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>This is a <strong>(fractional) weighted vertex cover</strong>: assign weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> to the vertices so that each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> has its endpoints' weights summing to at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, while minimizing the total weight assigned. In the classic unweighted vertex cover we pick a set of vertices touching every edge; here each edge instead demands a real amount <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> that must be met by the amounts placed on its two endpoints, and the variables are allowed to be fractional because this is the LP (not the integer program). This matches the official phrasing: put enough resource at the vertices so that for each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> the amount at its endpoints is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#d": "<p>The simplex algorithm needs every inequality turned into an equality by adding a nonnegative slack/surplus variable, after which the \"slack\" variables are expressed as functions of the original ones (the dictionary/slack form used to read off a starting vertex).</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>The first constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math> is a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" inequality. Subtract a nonnegative surplus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> to make it an equality: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>The second constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math> is a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" inequality. Add a nonnegative slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Keeping all variables nonnegative, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the standard (equality) form is\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;s.t.&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>Now solve each equation for its slack to get the dictionary form (slacks basic, original variables nonbasic). From line one, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>; from line two, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>7</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>This is exactly the form simplex runs on: the basic variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are written in terms of the nonbasic <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, so setting the nonbasic variables to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> reads off a candidate vertex.</p>\n", "2016A-p6#e": "<p>A simplex \"basis\" names which variables are allowed to be nonzero; the remaining (nonbasic) variables are set to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and the basic ones are solved for from the two equality constraints. Here the basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>There are five variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Since the basis is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, the <em>nonbasic</em> variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, and we set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Use the second equality constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> leaves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Use the first equality constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>. Substituting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>; with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> this is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>Both basic variables came out nonnegative (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>), so this vertex is feasible. (Equivalently, from the dictionary form of part (d): <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>11</mn></mrow></math>.) The basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> therefore corresponds to the feasible vertex above.</p>\n", "2017A-p1#a": "<p>The algorithm makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> recursive calls, each on an input of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. The non-recursive work is the divide step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> plus the combine step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> dominates <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the total work outside the recursion is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This is exactly the recurrence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which the master theorem resolves.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Compute the &quot;watershed&quot; exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Numerically <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.79</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02248;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>0.79</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Because the exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> grows polynomially faster than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>: concretely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. This is <strong>Case 3</strong> of the master theorem.</li>\n<li>Verify the regularity condition needed for Case 3: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for some constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so it holds.</li>\n<li>Case 3 therefore gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The base case (inputs of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>500</mn></mrow></math> solved directly in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> time) does not change the asymptotics: it applies only for inputs of bounded size, so each direct solve costs at most a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>500</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Answer: the running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</div>", "2017A-p1#b": "<p>Each level of recursion halves both dimensions and does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work; write that per-level cost as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Unrolling the recurrence sums these costs down the single chain of calls, and the key point is that the sizes shrink geometrically, so the sum is a convergent geometric series.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>At the top level the work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The recursive call is on an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>&#x0230A;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x0230B;</mi></mrow></math> matrix, whose work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The next level contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and so on, each level being half of the previous one.</li>\n<li>Summing over all levels:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li>The bracket is a geometric series with ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so it is bounded: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></msub><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Hence\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li>The lower bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is immediate because the very first level already does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work. Together this gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The base case (a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix handled in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time) is consistent with this bound, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; it is reached after about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> levels but costs no more than one term of the series.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Answer: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</div>", "2017A-p1#c": "<p>The statement is <strong>True</strong>, and the reason is linearity of expectation.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Linearity of expectation says that for any random variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> on the same sample space and any constants <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Applying it with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>The crucial point the problem is testing: this identity requires <strong>no</strong> independence assumption. Linearity follows directly from the definition of expectation as a sum (or integral) over the sample space,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhich is just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. No term ever requires the joint distribution to factor.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Independence is needed only for statements about <em>products</em>, such as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>; it is irrelevant for linear combinations. So the equation holds for arbitrary, possibly dependent, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>", "2017A-p1#e": "<p>The statement is <strong>True</strong>. Recall the definition: an algorithm is a <em>polynomial-time approximation scheme (PTAS)</em> for a minimization problem <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math> if for every fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> it produces a solution of cost at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times the optimum, in time polynomial in the input size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> (the running time may depend arbitrarily on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>).</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>The approximation guarantee is satisfied by hypothesis: for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> the algorithm returns a solution whose quality is within a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of optimal.</li>\n<li>The running-time requirement is also met. Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> as a constant. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> is a constant, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for the constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> &mdash; a polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The PTAS definition allows the <em>exponent</em> to blow up as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; it only requires polynomiality in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for each fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Both conditions hold, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is a PTAS for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math>. Note the algorithm is <em>not</em> an FPTAS (fully polynomial scheme), since that would additionally require the running time to be polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>, whereas here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> sits in the exponent. But the question only asks about PTAS, so the answer is True.</p>", "2017A-p3#x": "<p>The claim is <strong>true</strong>: this Christofides variant is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math>-approximation for metric Path-TSP. Recall the algorithm: build a minimum spanning tree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> be its odd-degree vertices; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be a minimum-cost matching pairing all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> <em>except exactly two</em> vertices; find an Euler path in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> and shortcut repeats into a Hamilton path. Let OPT be the optimal Hamilton path.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>An Euler path exists.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is nonempty (every leaf of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, hence is odd). Adding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> flips the parity of every matched vertex, so in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> exactly the two unmatched vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> remain odd; all others are even. A connected graph with exactly two odd-degree vertices has an Euler path (the hint), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> has one.</li>\n<li><strong>Bounding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> OPT is a Hamilton path, which is in particular a spanning tree; since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> is a <em>minimum</em> spanning tree, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Bounding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> As shown in class, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is even; write its vertices as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> in the order they appear along OPT. Form two matchings:\n<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Each of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> leaves exactly two vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> unmatched, so each is a candidate for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Triangle inequality.</strong> Along OPT the vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> appear in order, so OPT contains vertex-disjoint subpaths <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. By the triangle inequality each matching edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> costs at most the length of the OPT subpath between them. The edges of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> together use each of these subpaths at most once, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Pick the cheaper.</strong> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> is a minimum such matching, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>M</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">t</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Shortcutting repeated vertices only removes cost (triangle inequality), so the returned Hamilton path costs at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, proving the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1.5</mn></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n", "2017A-p4#x": "<p>We sample bits from the stream and output the sample mean as our estimate of the true fraction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math> of ones. We must choose the number of samples <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> so that the estimate is within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>. Chernoff tells us how large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> must be.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Setup.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for whether the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th sampled bit is a one; the samples are independent, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>. The estimate is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, with mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chernoff.</strong> Using the multiplicative (additive-in-this-form) Chernoff bound for the deviation of the sample mean,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> covers both tails (estimate too high or too low).</li>\n<li><strong>Impose the confidence requirement.</strong> We need this failure probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0.9</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0.05</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nTaking logs, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>30000</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></mfrac></mrow></math> because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0.01</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>30000</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Solve for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> without knowing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Rearranging gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>30000</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> always, it suffices to take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>ln</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>20</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>30000</mn></mrow></math>, which guarantees the bound for every possible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BC;</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mn>30000</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>20</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math> samples (a fixed constant, about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>90000</mn></mrow></math>, independent of the stream length) suffice to estimate the fraction of ones within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.01</mn></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2017A-p6#a": "<p>We must express &quot;pick a smallest subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> that meets every set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>&quot; as a binary integer program. The natural decision variables are one indicator per element of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means &quot;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is placed in the hitting set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>&quot; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is left out. The chosen set is then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>The hitting requirement is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. at least one element of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is selected. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> counts how many elements of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> are selected, &quot;at least one&quot; becomes the linear constraint\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li>The objective is to minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Putting it together gives the binary integer program\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nEach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> variable forces an all-or-nothing choice per element, and the constraints exactly encode &quot;hit every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>&quot;, so optimal solutions are precisely minimum hitting sets.</p>", "2017A-p6#b": "<p>Start from the LP relaxation of part (a): replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Drop the redundant upper bounds.</strong> Because we are minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and every &quot;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>&quot; constraint has nonnegative coefficients, no optimal solution ever needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: lowering any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> keeps all constraints satisfied and does not increase the objective. So the constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> can be removed, leaving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Form the dual.</strong> This is a minimization with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> constraints and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, whose dual is a maximization with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> constraints and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Introduce one dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> per primal constraint (one per set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li><strong>Dual objective.</strong> The primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the dual objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Dual constraints (one per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>).</strong> The primal objective coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> appears in constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>. Collecting its column gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Dual: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mstyle></mrow></math></div>", "2017A-p6#c": "<p>Take the dual from part (b) and impose integrality, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, to get the binary integer program\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nWe read off what this asks combinatorially.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, the variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the indicator of &quot;subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is chosen&quot;. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D49F;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> be the chosen collection.</li>\n<li>The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>D</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>&#x1D49F;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is the number of chosen subsets, which we <em>maximize</em>.</li>\n<li>The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>D</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> says that each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> belongs to <em>at most one</em> chosen subset. Equivalently, no two chosen subsets share an element &mdash; the chosen subsets are pairwise disjoint.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The BIP is the <strong>set packing</strong> problem: find a maximum-size collection of pairwise disjoint (mutually non-overlapping) subsets from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</div>", "2017A-p6#d": "<p>Standard form here means a maximization <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> whose constraints are all &quot;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>&quot; inequalities and whose variables are all sign-constrained to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The given LP violates this in three ways: it has an equality, and two variables constrained <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. We fix each.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Flip the nonpositive variables.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> with the new <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; likewise <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> with new <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is already fine. After substitution every variable satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Rewrite the objective.</strong> Under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, the objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Split the equality into two inequalities.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> is equivalent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Applying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, and the &quot;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>&quot; half <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> is turned into a &quot;<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>&quot; by negating: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Convert the remaining inequality.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, already of the correct form.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Standard form:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>5</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mrow><mi>&</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>3</mn><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></div>", "2017B-p2#a": "<p>To show the ratio-sorted greedy has <em>unbounded</em> approximation ratio, we exhibit one family of instances on which greedy's value is a factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> below optimal — and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> can be arbitrarily large, so no constant bound holds.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: sorting by density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> can make greedy grab a tiny high-density item that then blocks a much more valuable heavy item.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>The instance.</strong> Two items and capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>: item 1 has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; item 2 has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Densities.</strong> Item 1 has ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; item 2 has ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, greedy sorts item 1 first.</li>\n<li><strong>What greedy does.</strong> It adds item 1 (weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, fine). Now the remaining capacity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which is less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, so item 2 no longer fits and is rejected. Greedy's total cost is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>What OPT does.</strong> Taking item 2 alone uses weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> and yields cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>The ratio.</strong> The approximation ratio on this instance is at least\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">g</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">y</mi></mrow></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>, this ratio grows without bound. Hence for any claimed constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C1;</mi></mrow></math>, choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x003C1;</mi></mrow></math> breaks it, so the greedy algorithm has no bounded approximation ratio.</p>\n", "2017B-p2#b": "<p>We write knapsack as a binary integer linear program (BIP). Recall the input: items <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> with cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and a capacity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>; we want a subset of maximum total cost whose total weight is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Decision variables.</strong> Introduce one variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> per item, forced to be integral in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means \"item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is chosen,\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means \"not chosen.\" A 0/1 vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is exactly a subset of items.</li>\n<li><strong>Objective.</strong> The total cost of the chosen set is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> (each chosen item contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, each unchosen contributes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). We maximize it.</li>\n<li><strong>Capacity constraint.</strong> The total weight is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; it must not exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Putting it together:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>The integrality constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is what makes this an exact model of the combinatorial problem: it forbids taking a fraction of an item.</p>\n", "2017B-p2#c": "<p>The LP relaxation is obtained from the BIP of part (b) by <em>relaxing</em> the integrality constraint: we keep the objective and the capacity constraint but let each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> take any real value in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> instead of only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</strong> The discrete set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is exactly the integer points of the interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>; dropping integrality means allowing every value in between. We write the interval as two linear inequalities: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Everything else is unchanged.</strong> Objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> are already linear, so they carry over verbatim.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The relaxation is therefore\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<p>Why do this: the integer program is NP-hard, but this LP is solvable in polynomial time, and its optimum is an upper bound on the integer optimum (any feasible 0/1 solution is also LP-feasible). Interpreting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> as \"the fraction of item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> taken\" makes this the <em>fractional knapsack</em>.</p>\n", "2017B-p2#d": "<p>We form the LP dual of the relaxation from part (c). Mechanically, each <em>primal constraint</em> becomes a <em>dual variable</em>, and each <em>primal variable</em> becomes a <em>dual constraint</em>; a max-primal becomes a min-dual.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>List the primal constraints and assign dual variables.</strong> The primal (in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>) is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to the single capacity row <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> and the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> upper bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Give the capacity row a dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and each upper bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> a dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. (Both are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> because these are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math> constraints in a maximization.)</li>\n<li><strong>Dual objective = combination of the primal right-hand sides.</strong> The capacity RHS is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi></mrow></math> and each upper-bound RHS is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so we minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>W</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>One dual constraint per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</strong> Collect the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> across the primal constraints: it appears with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the capacity row (multiplied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>) and coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in its own bound (multiplied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>). Their sum must be at least the objective coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Sign constraints.</strong> Since the primal variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the dual constraints are inequalities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> (not equalities), and the dual variables satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The dual is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>W</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n", "2017B-p3#x": "<p>We are told the cover time of a graph with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> edges is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and we must show a walk from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> reaches a specific vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. The tool is Markov's inequality applied to the hitting time.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: reaching one target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is easier than covering everything, so the expected time to hit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is bounded by the cover time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Then Markov turns that expectation into a tail bound at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo></mrow></math> the mean.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Define the random variable.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> be the number of steps until the walk first reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> (the hitting time of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li><strong>Bound its expectation.</strong> The cover time is the expected time to visit <em>all</em> vertices; visiting all vertices in particular visits <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. Hence the expected time to reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is at most the cover time: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Apply Markov.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so Markov's inequality with threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> gives\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Complement.</strong> Therefore the walk reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>3</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The choice <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is exactly what makes Markov yield the clean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> failure bound.</p>\n", "2017B-p4#a": "<p>We must show that for any set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> three-literal clauses there is an assignment satisfying at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> of them. The proof pairs each assignment with its bitwise complement.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>The pairing.</strong> Fix any assignment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> be its negation: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> assigns to each variable the opposite value it has in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. We compare how many clauses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> satisfies with how many <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies.</li>\n  <li><strong>Every clause is caught by one of them.</strong> Take any clause, a disjunction of three literals. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> does <em>not</em> satisfy it, then all three of its literals are false under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. Passing to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> flips every variable, hence flips every literal to true, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies that clause. Therefore each clause is satisfied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math>, or by both — never by neither.</li>\n  <li><strong>Count.</strong> Summing over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> clauses, <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>clauses&#x000A0;satisfied&#x000A0;by&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>clauses&#x000A0;satisfied&#x000A0;by&#x000A0;</mtext><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div> because each clause contributes at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> to the left side.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">If two nonnegative numbers sum to at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, the larger is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So at least one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x000AF;</mo></mover></mrow></math> satisfies at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> clauses, proving that such an assignment always exists.</div>\n", "2018A-p1#a": "<p>Set up the recurrence from Strassen's structure, then apply the Master Theorem.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Recurrence.</strong> On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> inputs Strassen makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> recursive multiplications of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> matrices, giving the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> term. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> additions of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> matrices (plus splitting/combining) each cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> elementwise operations, so the non-recursive work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></li>\n<li><strong>Master Theorem form</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. Compare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math>, the \"leaf-heavy\" case, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Evaluate the exponent:</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mi>b</mi></msub><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.807</mn></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>, which is below the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> of naive multiplication.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Intuitively: the number of subproblems grows like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>7</mn><mrow><mtext>depth</mtext></mrow></msup></mrow></math> while each shrinks by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> in size-work, so the bottom level (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> leaves) dominates the total.</p>\n", "2018A-p1#b": "<p>The recurrence is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>T</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>T</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> split cost dominates the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> combine cost, so the local work at a node of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This is <em>not</em> a Master-Theorem form (unequal subproblem sizes), so reason level by level via the recursion tree.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: the total subproblem size shrinks by the constant factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>28</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>30</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> each level, and because work is superlinear (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>) the levels form a decreasing geometric series dominated by the top.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Level work.</strong> The root does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Its children have sizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; their combined work is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>5</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>6</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>36</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>0.70</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Geometric decay.</strong> Squaring makes each level a constant factor <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>25</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>36</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> of the level above, so total work <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. (Depth is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> since sizes shrink by a constant factor, but the sum converges regardless.)</li>\n<li><strong>Lower bound.</strong> The root alone costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With the matching upper bound, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2018A-p1#e": "<p>Answer: <strong>Monte Carlo.</strong> The distinction is about which resource is fixed versus random:</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>A <em>Las Vegas</em> algorithm always returns a correct answer; only its running time is random.</li>\n<li>A <em>Monte Carlo</em> algorithm has a fixed (bounded) running time but may return a wrong answer with some probability.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Karger's randomized min-cut contracts a uniformly random edge repeatedly until two vertices remain, outputting the surviving cut. It always halts within the same number of contractions (bounded runtime), but it can output a non-minimum cut: a single run finds a fixed minimum cut only with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, because it succeeds only if it never contracts an edge of that cut. Fixed time, possibly wrong answer — that is Monte Carlo. (Repeating <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times drives the failure probability down, but each run is still Monte Carlo.)</p>\n", "2018A-p2#a": "<p>We want a hitting set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>: a set meeting every subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> (that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>), using as few elements as possible. When every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, a simple greedy achieves a factor-3 approximation. The algorithm mirrors the maximal-matching idea behind the vertex-cover 2-approximation.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Algorithm: start with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. Process the subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> in arbitrary order. When you reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> does not yet hit it (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>), add <em>all</em> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02190;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, do nothing and move on.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Why it returns a valid hitting set: consider any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> after the algorithm ends. Either it was already hit when we examined it (so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>), or it was not, in which case we added all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, again making <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. So every subset is hit.</li>\n<li>Why \"add all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>\" is affordable: each time we add, we insert at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> elements, and the sets we add are pairwise disjoint (we only add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> when it is currently unhit, so it shares no element with any previously added set). This disjointness is what powers the approximation bound proved in part (b).</li>\n<li>Polynomial time: for each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> subsets we test <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, which is cheap, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> stays bounded by the total number of elements appearing in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the greedy runs in polynomial time and outputs a hitting set; part (b) shows its size is within a factor 3 of optimal.</p>\n", "2018A-p2#b": "<p>We prove three things: the algorithm runs in polynomial time, its output is a hitting set, and its size is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. The structure copies the vertex-cover 2-approximation proof.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Polynomial time.</strong> Represent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> as a linked list. Testing whether <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> for a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>) takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time. Since we only ever add elements that belong to some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mo>&#x022C3;</mo><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is bounded by the input size and each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> iterations is polynomial. Hence the whole algorithm is polynomial.</li>\n<li><strong>Correctness (it is a hitting set).</strong> By the \"otherwise do nothing\" branch, whenever we finished processing a subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> we had <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>: either it was already hit, or we forced it by adding all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>. So every subset ends up hit.</li>\n<li><strong>Approximation ratio.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of subsets on which the \"add\" condition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> fired. Two counts:\n<ul>\n<li><em>Upper bound on our output.</em> Each firing adds at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> elements, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><em>Lower bound on OPT.</em> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> subsets we added are pairwise disjoint: we add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> only when it currently shares no element with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> already contains every previously added set. A single element can therefore hit at most one of these <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> disjoint subsets. Any hitting set, in particular an optimal one, must contain at least one element from each, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ul>\n</li>\n<li>Combine: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Thus the greedy is a polynomial-time 3-approximation for minimum hitting set when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2018A-p2#c": "<p>In the online version the subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> arrive one at a time; after each arrival <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> must already hit everything seen so far, and once an element is added it can never be removed. We must be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-competitive: our final <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> times the optimal hitting set for the whole (final) instance.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Online algorithm: keep the greedy from part (a). When a new subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> arrives, check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>, add all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02190;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>. Otherwise leave <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> unchanged.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>The key observation is that the offline greedy of part (a) already processes subsets in an <em>arbitrary</em> order and never revisits or removes a decision. Its analysis did not rely on knowing future subsets.</li>\n<li>Therefore we may let \"arbitrary order\" be exactly the online arrival order. Each arriving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is handled with the same rule, using only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> as it currently stands.</li>\n<li>The requirement is met at every step: after we process an arriving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02229;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math> (either it was already hit, or we just added all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> hits all subsets seen so far. Decisions are irrevocable, matching the online rules.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Since the final <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> is identical to what the offline greedy would produce on that sequence, part (b)'s bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> applies verbatim. Hence the same greedy is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-competitive online algorithm.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#a": "<p>We model minimum-cost perfect matching as an integer linear program (ILP) with one binary decision variable per edge.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Variable meaning: for each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is chosen for the matching and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is not.</div>\n<p>With these variables, \"cost of the chosen matching\" is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and we minimize it. The remaining job is to force the chosen edge set to be a <em>perfect</em> matching, i.e. every vertex is covered by exactly one chosen edge.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>For a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the edges incident to it are the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>. The number of chosen edges touching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Perfect matching means each vertex is met by exactly one matched edge, so we impose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>. Equality (not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) is what makes it perfect rather than just a matching: it forbids leaving a vertex uncovered, and being <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> simultaneously forbids two matched edges at a vertex.</li>\n<li>Integrality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> makes each edge either fully in or fully out.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The complete ILP is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nAny feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is exactly the indicator of a perfect matching, and its objective is that matching's weight, so an optimal solution is a minimum-cost perfect matching.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#b": "<p>The LP relaxation is obtained by replacing the integrality constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with the real interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The task then asks us to drop any constraint that is implied by the others.</p>\n<p>Naively the relaxation would read\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nWe claim the upper bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> are redundant.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Take any edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Its endpoint constraint at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, a sum that <em>includes</em> the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Every term in that sum is nonnegative, because the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is kept. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> already follows from the vertex equality plus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; writing it explicitly adds nothing and can be removed.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Removing the redundant upper bounds gives the clean relaxation\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThis is the standard matching-polytope LP: only the vertex equalities and nonnegativity remain.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#c": "<p>We take the dual of the relaxed primal from part (b):\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThere is one primal constraint per vertex, so the dual has one variable per vertex.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Dual variables: for each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Because its primal constraint is an <em>equality</em>, the dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <em>unconstrained</em> in sign (free), rather than restricted to be nonnegative.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Objective.</strong> The primal right-hand sides are all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (the vertex equalities <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>). The dual maximizes the inner product of right-hand sides with dual variables: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Constraints, one per primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</strong> The primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> appears in exactly two vertex constraints, those of its endpoints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, each with coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Its primal objective coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. A primal minimization with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> yields a dual \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraint, so for edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Sign of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</strong> An equality primal constraint dualizes to a free variable, so each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> is unconstrained.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The dual is therefore\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>u</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>free</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nIntuitively each vertex is assigned a \"potential\" <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>; every edge caps the sum of its endpoints' potentials by its weight, and we maximize total potential.</p>\n", "2018A-p3#d": "<p>The obstacle is that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is not a linear function of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is not directly an LP. The standard trick is to introduce an auxiliary variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> that stands for the absolute value from above.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key identity: for any real <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>, the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> equals the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> satisfying both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>. Minimizing such a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> therefore reproduces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>. Impose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>. Together these say <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Make <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> the objective: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>. At the optimum the minimization pushes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> down until one of the two constraints is tight, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>; minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is thus equivalent to minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. We do not need a sign constraint on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> because the two inequalities already force <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Keep the original feasibility constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> unchanged; it is already linear.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The resulting linear program is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nEvery constraint and the objective are linear in the variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, so this is a valid LP whose optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>10</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2018B-p2#a": "<p>Approximation ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> would mean the greedy always returns a <em>maximum</em> independent set. To refute this we exhibit, for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, a graph where the min-degree greedy is fooled into stopping after picking just two vertices, while a much larger independent set exists.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>The graph.</strong> On <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> put edges: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> (a star from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>'s); <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> (complete bipartite between <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>'s and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>'s); and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> (a clique on the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>'s).</li>\n  <li><strong>Degrees.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> (just the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>'s). <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> edges to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>'s plus one to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> edges to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>'s plus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> inside the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>-clique. For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is the unique minimum-degree vertex.</li>\n  <li><strong>What greedy does.</strong> It first takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> (min degree) and deletes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> together with all its neighbors, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>'s. What remains is just the clique <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. It then takes some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and deletes it and all its neighbors — the entire rest of the clique — so the graph empties and the algorithm halts, returning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The optimum is far bigger.</strong> There are no edges among the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>'s, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is an independent set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\nThe greedy returns an independent set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so the ratio is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, which grows without bound. The claim of approximation ratio <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is false.\n</div>\n", "2018B-p2#b": "<p>When every vertex has degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math>, the same greedy is a polynomial-time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-approximation. The proof has three ingredients: correctness (the output really is independent), a counting bound relating the output size to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, and a trivial upper bound on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Output is independent.</strong> Each time a vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is added to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>'s neighbors are immediately deleted from the graph, so no later-chosen vertex can be adjacent to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. Hence no two vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> are adjacent.</li>\n  <li><strong>Upper bound on the optimum.</strong> An independent set is a subset of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, so trivially <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Counting iterations.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of loop iterations. Each iteration adds exactly one vertex to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, so the returned set has size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. Each iteration deletes the chosen vertex plus its remaining neighbors — at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi></mrow></math> vertices in total. The loop runs until the graph is empty, i.e. until all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> vertices have been deleted, so the deletions must account for everything: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Combine.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, which rearranges to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. That is exactly a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-approximation.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&#x027F9;</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x00394;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>Running time.</strong> There are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> iterations. Keeping the vertices' current degrees in a heap lets us extract a minimum-degree vertex, delete it and its neighbors, and update the affected degrees, all in polynomial time per iteration — so the whole algorithm is polynomial.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#a": "<p>The answer is <strong>Yes</strong>, and the reason is worth spelling out because it is exactly the point of randomization.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: the randomness lives in the algorithm's coin flips, not in the input. So the expected-time guarantee holds for <em>every</em> input, including the adversary's worst one.</div>\n<p>For a <em>deterministic</em> algorithm, \"worst case\" and \"average case\" refer to averaging over inputs, so a bound proved on random inputs need not survive on the single nastiest input. Randomized selection is different: for a fixed input array, the running time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is still a random variable, but the source of that randomness is the algorithm's own uniformly random pivot choices, not any assumption about the array.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Fix any input array <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, even one chosen adversarially to be worst possible.</li>\n<li>The analysis bounds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> where the expectation is taken over the internal coin flips (pivot selections). Nowhere does it assume the array elements are random or ordered in a lucky way.</li>\n<li>Hence the bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a statement about the worst-case input, averaged over the algorithm's randomness.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So yes: the expected-running-time analysis applies to the worst-case input array. An adversary who sees the array but not the future coin flips cannot force bad behavior in expectation.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#b": "<p>The expectation is taken <strong>over the random choices of pivots made by the algorithm</strong>.</p>\n<p>This is the natural follow-up to part (a). When we write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, the running time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a random variable, and we must be clear about what probability space it lives in.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>The input array is treated as <em>fixed</em> (arbitrary, possibly worst-case). It contributes no randomness.</li>\n<li>The only random events are the pivot selections: at each recursive call the algorithm picks a pivot uniformly at random among the current elements.</li>\n<li>These independent uniform choices determine which subarray we recurse on and hence how much work is done, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a function of the pivot sequence. Averaging <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> over all equally likely pivot sequences gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>In short, the randomness comes entirely from the coin flips inside the algorithm, which is precisely why the expectation bound is valid on every input.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#c": "<p>We must justify the recurrence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The idea: condition on which element becomes the pivot, then bound each case by the largest subproblem it could create.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Condition on the pivot's rank.</strong> The pivot is uniform, so for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> it is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th largest element with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Partitioning costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> regardless.</li>\n<li><strong>Bound the recursive call.</strong> If the pivot is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th largest, the two sides have sizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. Selection recurses on only one side, but we don't know which; to get an upper bound we pessimistically assume it recurses on the <em>larger</em> side, costing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Average over the pivot.</strong> By the law of total expectation,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>max</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Collapse the max.</strong> As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> runs over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the two arguments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> each range over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The larger of the pair is always some index in the upper half <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>&#x02308;</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x02309;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Each such index is hit by at most two values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> (once as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, once as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>), so the sum of maxima is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Substituting gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, as required. (Rounding of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> is ignored per the instructions.)</p>\n", "2018B-p4#d": "<p>We prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for a suitable constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>; this immediately gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> be the constant hidden in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> partition cost, so that term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Inductive hypothesis.</strong> Assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Base case.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> be a constant with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. Choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the claim holds at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Inductive step.</strong> Apply part (c)'s recurrence, then the hypothesis on each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Sum the arithmetic series.</strong> The terms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> form an arithmetic series. Using (first + last) × (half the count): first <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, last <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and there are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> terms, giving\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Simplify.</strong> Then\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Close the induction.</strong> We need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn><mi>c</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; both requirements hold.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2018B-p4#e": "<p>We are given a Monte Carlo view: the selection algorithm runs in expected <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations. We want a version that <em>always</em> stops within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>25</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations and is correct with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The trick is capped repetition plus Markov.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: a run that goes over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps is \"unlucky.\" By Markov each run is unlucky with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>; make the runs independent and 5 of them all fail with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Cap each run.</strong> Run the algorithm, but abort a run as soon as its step counter reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Do this at most 5 times, using fresh independent randomness each time.</li>\n<li><strong>Output rule.</strong> If some run finishes within its <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> budget, return its (correct) answer. If all 5 runs are aborted, return the first array element (a possibly wrong guess).</li>\n<li><strong>Worst-case operation count.</strong> Each run does at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, so five runs do at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps. With the counter bookkeeping this stays within the required <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>25</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations — always, deterministically. So it is Las Vegas in time (bounded) and Monte Carlo only in correctness... actually the reverse: bounded worst-case time, small error probability.</li>\n<li><strong>Failure only if all runs overshoot.</strong> The output is wrong only when every run exceeded <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps, since any run that finishes returns the exact correct selection.</li>\n<li><strong>Markov on one run.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be one run's step count, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Markov gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></li>\n<li><strong>Independence across runs.</strong> The 5 runs use independent coins, so\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>all&#x000A0;5&#x000A0;overshoot</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">(</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">)</mo><mn>5</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Therefore the algorithm returns the correct answer with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> while never exceeding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>25</mn><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> operations.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#1": "<p>We are given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> students <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> courses; course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is attended by the subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and comes with a cap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. We want to select as many students as possible subject to: for every course, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> of the selected students who attend that course may be chosen. Modeling this as a 0/1 integer program (BIP) means choosing binary decision variables, an objective, and one linear constraint per rule.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Variables.</strong> Introduce one binary variable per student: let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is selected and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is not. Binary values are what make each student an all-or-nothing choice.</li>\n  <li><strong>Objective.</strong> We want the largest possible selected group, i.e. to maximize the count of selected students. Since each selected student contributes exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the count is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and we maximize it.</li>\n  <li><strong>Constraints.</strong> The cap is per course. For a fixed course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, the students attending it are exactly those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so the number selected among them is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Requiring \"at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>\" is the linear inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, one such constraint for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>A student attending several courses appears in several of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> uses up one unit of each of those courses' caps simultaneously — which is exactly the intended \"counts as a representative for each\" behavior.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#2": "<p>For the concrete instance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and caps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, we first encode \"who attends what\" in a matrix, write the primal compactly, and then form its dual by the standard rule for a packing program.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Membership matrix.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrix with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Reading off each student's courses gives the rows\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x00028;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>row&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>student&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>col&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>course&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n  Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>M</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, exactly the count of selected attendees of course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Primal in matrix form.</strong> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and objective vector all-ones, part 1 becomes\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mover><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><strong>Dual.</strong> For a packing LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, the dual is the covering LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>M</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with one dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> per primal constraint (per course). Restricting to integers gives the dual BIP.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>M</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mover><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\">&#x02192;</mo></mover><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>Each of the five rows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> is one student's covering constraint (e.g. row 1: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), matching the interpretation developed in part 3.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#3": "<p>Here we write down the dual BIP of the general primal from part 1 and give it a combinatorial meaning. Because the primal has one constraint per course, the dual has one variable per course; because the primal has one variable per student, the dual has one constraint per student. Reading the transposed system this way turns a <em>packing</em> problem for students into a <em>covering</em> problem for courses.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Variables.</strong> The dual has a binary variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for each course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means course <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is chosen for the solution. Its cost coefficient is the primal right-hand side <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Objective.</strong> The primal maximizes attendance and has right-hand sides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>; by duality these become the dual objective coefficients, and the direction flips to minimization: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the total cost of the chosen courses.</li>\n  <li><strong>Constraints.</strong> Transposing the constraint matrix, each primal variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> (student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>) yields one dual constraint. Student <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> appears in the primal columns for exactly the courses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so the dual constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: every student must be enrolled in at least one chosen course.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>In words: choose a set of courses of minimum total cost so that every student is covered by (enrolled in) at least one chosen course. This is precisely a weighted set-cover: the courses are the sets, the students are the elements to be covered, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the cost of using set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2021A-p2#4": "<p>The claim is that when all caps are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and all courses have the same size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, solving the LP relaxation of the primal optimally and then rounding \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>u</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" always gives a <em>feasible</em> integral solution. This is <strong>false</strong>; the fix is to exhibit an instance where the optimal fractional solution sits exactly at the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, so rounding pushes too many variables up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and breaks a cap.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Simplest counterexample.</strong> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The only constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The fractional optimum sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; it is optimal because the single constraint caps the sum at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so no feasible point beats value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Apply the rounding: the threshold is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and both variables equal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so both round to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Now <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, violating the cap — the rounded solution is infeasible.</li>\n  <li><strong>A second, \"tighter\" counterexample.</strong> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x029F5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> and every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is feasible (each constraint sums <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>; it is optimal because any higher-value solution would force three variables to sum to more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which some constraint forbids.</li>\n  <li>Rounding with threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>: every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> rounds to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Then each constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>U</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, again infeasible.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\nFalse: in both instances the optimal fractional solution puts every variable exactly at the rounding threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>, so all of them round up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and every cap-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> constraint is violated.\n</div>\n", "2021A-p3#1": "<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is a complete graph whose every edge costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and we want a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>-approximation for TSP. The first step is given: since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is complete it has a cycle cover, so let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> be a <em>minimum-cost cycle cover</em> — a set of vertex-disjoint cycles that together visit every vertex exactly once, of least total edge cost (computable in polynomial time). We must finish the algorithm by turning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> into a single tour.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Fix an arbitrary order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> of the cycles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Write the vertices of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> around the cycle as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the edge that closes cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, do not close cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>: delete its closing edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and instead add the edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> jumping to the first vertex of the next cycle.</li>\n  <li>For the last cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>, replace its closing edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>l</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, an edge back to the very first vertex, which closes everything into one big cycle.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is complete, every replacement edge exists. Each cycle contributes its path of vertices, and the new \"bridge\" edges chain the cycles <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>. The result is a single closed cycle passing through every vertex exactly once, i.e. a Hamiltonian tour — a valid TSP solution. Its cost bound is proved in part (2).</p>\n", "2021A-p3#2": "<p>We bound the cost of the tour built in part (1) from the minimum cycle cover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext></mrow></math> for its cost and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math> for the optimal TSP cost; recall every edge costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>It is a tour.</strong> Merging the cycles as in (1) (drop each cycle's closing edge, bridge to the next, and close the last back to the first) produces one cycle through every vertex exactly once, so the output is a legal TSP tour.</li>\n  <li><strong>Few cycles.</strong> A cycle in a simple graph has at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices, and the cycles of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> are vertex-disjoint and cover all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices, so their number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cheap patching.</strong> Building the tour performs one edge replacement per cycle. Each swaps out an edge of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for an edge of cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so it raises the cost by at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> replacements the total increase is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lower bounds on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.</strong> First, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>: the optimal tour is itself a cycle cover (a single cycle covering all vertices), so the minimum cycle cover is no costlier. Second, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>: any tour has exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> edges, each costing at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>OPT</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>where the middle step uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>cost</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Hence the algorithm is a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>-approximation for TSP on these instances.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#1": "<p>Recall the setup: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is bipartite with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0222A;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. A <em>semi-matching</em> is an edge set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> in which every vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, while every vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. We want the largest such <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>E</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and we model it as a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> program.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>For each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> introduce a binary variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is chosen for the solution and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is not.</li>\n  <li>\"Largest semi-matching\" means maximizing the number of chosen edges, so the objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>The degree cap on a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: the chosen edges touching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so we require <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>The degree cap on a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>: similarly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. There is exactly one constraint per vertex, and a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>-vertex is capped at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>Any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector respects both degree caps, hence is the indicator of a semi-matching, and its objective is that set's size; so an optimal solution is a maximum semi-matching.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#2": "<p>We instantiate part (1) for the drawn graph and then write its dual. The graph has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>e</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and edges <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>. Order the constraint rows as the vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Each row is a vertex; a row has a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in the columns of the edges incident to that vertex. Vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> touches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> touches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> touches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> touches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> touches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>e</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>The right-hand side is the degree cap: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> for the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>e</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The objective vector is all-ones (maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math>). With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> the matrix above, the primal is\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>A</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>The dual BIP swaps roles: a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> per row/vertex, a constraint per column/edge. The dual objective coefficients are the primal RHS <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the dual RHS is the primal objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and the constraint matrix is transposed:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>y</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because the exam asks for the dual <em>as a BIP</em>, the dual variables are also constrained to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; each dual constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"sans-serif\">T</mi></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> corresponds to one edge and forces at least one of its endpoints to be picked.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#3": "<p>We describe, in words, the general dual of the semi-matching BIP of part (1) and recognize it. The primal maximizes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> with a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" cap at each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex and a \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>\" cap at each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>The dual has one variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> per vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> (one per primal constraint). Read <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> as \"is vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> chosen for the solution?\".</li>\n  <li>The dual has one constraint per edge (one per primal variable). The column of edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>'s in the rows of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, so the constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: at least one endpoint of every edge must be selected. That is exactly the <strong>vertex cover</strong> condition.</li>\n  <li>The dual objective uses the primal right-hand sides as costs: a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex has cap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex has cap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so the cost of picking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>. We minimize the total cost, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>So the dual is <strong>weighted vertex cover</strong> where every vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> has weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and every vertex of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> has weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>: choose a minimum-weight set of vertices touching every edge.</p>\n", "2021B-p1#4": "<p>The claim is that solving the LP relaxation of the semi-matching program and rounding every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (and the rest to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) always yields a feasible integer semi-matching. This is <strong>false</strong>; we disprove it with one explicit counterexample.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: a \"star\" of four edges all meeting the single <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Consider the fractional point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for all four edges. Check feasibility of the relaxation: each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>-vertex is on one edge, contributing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is on all four, contributing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. So it is LP-feasible, with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>This is in fact LP-optimal: the constraint at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>e</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, so no feasible point can exceed value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>; ours attains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Now round: every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, so all four variables become <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. But then vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, violating its cap. The rounded vector is <em>not</em> a semi-matching.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence the rounding rule can turn a feasible fractional optimum into an infeasible integer point, so the claim fails. (Other witnesses work too, e.g. a triangle-free star <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.) The moral: threshold rounding at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> has no reason to respect capacity-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> constraints when many edges pile up on one vertex.</p>\n", "2021B-p3#1": "<p>We give a greedy algorithm for MAX-3-COL. Recall the goal: color the vertices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> so that the total weight of edges colored <em>legally</em> (endpoints differently colored) is as large as possible. Equivalently we partition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> into three color classes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> (containing the vertices colored <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>); an edge is legal iff its endpoints fall into different classes. The algorithm colors vertices one at a time, each time choosing the class that creates the least new illegal weight.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Initialize the three classes empty: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Process the vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> in an arbitrary fixed order. For the current vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and each class <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the total weight of edges connecting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to vertices <em>already placed</em> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Add <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to a class <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> for which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is minimal. (Placing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> in class <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> makes exactly the edges from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> illegal, since those endpoints now share <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>'s color, so we greedily minimize this newly created illegal weight.)</li>\n<li>After all vertices are placed, output the coloring: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">This is the natural three-color extension of the two-color greedy max-cut algorithm from Homework 2; the algorithm and its analysis are identical in structure.</div>\n", "2021B-p3#2": "<p>We prove the greedy algorithm achieves a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>-approximation: the legally colored weight is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> of the optimum. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be the vertices in the order they are processed, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> denote the three classes at the moment <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is considered (so they contain only vertices processed before <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>).</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Per-vertex bound.</strong> When <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is placed, it is added to the class minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The minimum of three numbers is at most their average, so the illegal weight created at this step is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Each edge counted once.</strong> An edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is accounted for exactly when the later endpoint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is processed—at that time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is already placed in one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Summing the three back-degrees over all vertices therefore counts every edge exactly once:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Total illegal weight.</strong> Summing the per-vertex bound in step 1 over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, the total weight of illegally colored edges is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Legal weight and comparison to OPT.</strong> Hence the legal weight is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Since no coloring can legalize more than all the edges, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, giving legal weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p1#a": "<p>The statement is <strong>false</strong>. The confusion is between a change of <em>basis</em> and a change of <em>vertex</em>. In the simplex algorithm as presented in class, a pivot is performed only when it <em>strictly decreases</em> the objective value; the algorithm never moves to a different vertex whose value merely ties the current one.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Each vertex (basic feasible solution) of the polytope corresponds to a basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>—a choice of columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> that determines the basic variables. A pivot swaps one column in and one out, giving a new basis.</li>\n<li>When the solution is <strong>degenerate</strong>, some basic variable equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and several different bases can describe the very same point. In that situation a pivot can change the basis while the vertex, and therefore the objective value, stays exactly the same. But this is not a move to a <em>different</em> vertex—it is the same geometric point.</li>\n<li>A genuine move to a different vertex changes the value of at least one variable and, in the class version of the pivot rule, is taken only when the objective strictly improves (decreases, for a minimization LP).</li>\n<li>Hence it is impossible for the algorithm to jump to a different vertex whose objective value equals the previous one: a different vertex is reached only with a strict decrease. The scenario described—equal value at a distinct vertex—does not occur.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p1#b": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. The original isolation lemma says: given a family <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> of subsets of a ground set, if each element gets an independent weight chosen uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and the weight of a set is the <em>sum</em> of its elements' weights, then with high probability there is a unique minimum-weight set. The trick is that the proof never uses the arithmetic values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> themselves—only that each element draws from a set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> distinct values, and that set-weight is monotone under changing one element's weight.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Suppose now the weight of a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is the <em>product</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, with the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> positive. Define a new weight function <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> is strictly increasing, the map <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a one-to-one, onto, order-preserving correspondence between weight functions valued in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and weight functions valued in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>log</mi><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. The latter is still a set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> distinct values.</li>\n<li>The logarithm turns products into sums: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>log</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><mi>log</mi><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So the product-weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> becomes the ordinary sum-weight of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Apply the original lemma to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math>: with high probability there is a unique set of minimal sum-weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi></mrow></math> is strictly monotone, the ordering of sets by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math> matches their ordering by the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>. Hence that same set is the unique minimum under the multiplicative definition.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p1#c": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003B2;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> means there is a polynomial-time randomized machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> with acceptance probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B2;</mi></mrow></math> on inputs in the language and at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math> on inputs outside it. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> demands the much stronger gap <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> vs. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>The easy inclusion <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> holds because, for large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>: a machine with tiny error already satisfies the weaker requirement.</li>\n<li>For the reverse inclusion, take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math>. The thresholds <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>8</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> are separated, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> is exactly their midpoint, so it cleanly distinguishes the two cases.</li>\n<li>Run <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> independently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> be the number of accepting runs. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>; if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. Accept iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>In either case the true acceptance rate is at distance <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> from the threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>16</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. By the Chernoff bound the probability that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> lands on the wrong side decays like <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> with a large enough constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> makes the error at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, so the amplified machine witnesses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p1#d": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. The naive algorithm multiplies two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrices with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>4</mn><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></math> scalar multiplications, but Strassen's method beats this. Recall Strassen multiplies two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices using only <strong>7</strong> multiplications (instead of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>), and crucially the seven products are combined using only additions and subtractions.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>View each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math> matrix as a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> block matrix whose four blocks are themselves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices. Matrix multiplication respects this block structure: the product's blocks are computed by the same formulas as for scalar <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> multiplication, treating blocks as \"entries.\"</li>\n<li>Apply Strassen at the block level. This produces the answer using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> block-multiplications (plus block additions/subtractions, which cost no scalar multiplications).</li>\n<li>Each of those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> operations is itself a product of two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> matrices. Compute each one with Strassen again, using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow></math> scalar multiplications.</li>\n<li>The total is therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow></math> scalar multiplications, i.e. one level of recursion of Strassen on top of the base <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> case. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>49</mn></mrow></math>, the bound is met.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">In general <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>k</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn></mrow></math>; here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>49</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>64</mn></mrow></math>.</div>\n", "2023A-p2#x": "<p>We model Minimum Dominating Set as a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> integer program. Recall <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> is dominating if every vertex is either in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> or adjacent to a vertex in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>; we want the smallest such <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Variables.</strong> For each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is selected into the dominating set\" and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means it is not. Any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> assignment thus encodes a candidate set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Objective.</strong> The size of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so we minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Domination constraints.</strong> For every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the domination requirement says <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> itself is chosen or at least one neighbor is chosen. As a linear inequality:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;all&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> covers \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>\"; the sum covers \"some neighbor of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>\". At least one of these must be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Full IP: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</div>\n<p><strong>Correspondence.</strong> Every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> solution gives a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; the constraint for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> guarantees <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> or has a neighbor in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> is a dominating set. Conversely any dominating set yields a feasible solution. Since the objective counts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, minimizing the IP finds a minimum dominating set.</p>\n", "2023A-p3#x": "<p>Maximum Triple Cut: partition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> into three sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> maximizing the number of edges whose endpoints land in different sets. We give a randomized <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>-approximation and note it derandomizes.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: with three parts, a random assignment separates any fixed edge's two endpoints with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Linearity of expectation turns this per-edge probability into an expected <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> of all edges cut.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Randomized algorithm.</strong> Independently assign each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math>, or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math>, each with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Probability an edge is cut.</strong> Fix edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Condition on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>'s set; whatever it is, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> lands in that same set with probability exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (its assignment is independent). So the endpoints differ — the edge crosses the cut — with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Expected cut size.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>e</mi></mrow></math> is cut, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. By linearity of expectation, the expected number of cut edges is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>e</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>e</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Approximation ratio.</strong> The optimum cuts at most every edge, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">A</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">G</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Derandomization.</strong> By the method of conditional expectations (as in class), fix the vertices one at a time: for each vertex choose the set among <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>V</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mrow></math> that maximizes the conditional expected cut given the choices so far. This never decreases the conditional expectation below its starting value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, yielding a deterministic assignment cutting at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> edges.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2023A-p4#a": "<p>We start from the matching integer program <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and integrality. Dropping the integrality constraint gives an LP; putting it in <em>standard form</em> means: a minimization objective, equality constraints, and all variables nonnegative — the form the simplex algorithm needs.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Maximize to minimize.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo>&#x02211;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>; negating the objective flips the sense without changing the optimizer.</li>\n  <li><strong>Inequalities to equalities.</strong> Each vertex constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> has slack, so add one nonnegative slack variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> per vertex: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Recovering the original: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>u</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is exactly the \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>\" condition, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> measures how far vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is from being saturated.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>s</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>Compactly, let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> incidence matrix — its column for edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> in rows <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> elsewhere. Writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, the program is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which is standard form.</p>\n", "2023A-p4#b": "<p>The primal from part (a) is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. We dualize it and then simplify.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>One dual variable per equality.</strong> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> equality constraints (one per vertex), and equality constraints produce <em>free</em> (unconstrained) dual variables, so the dual has a vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Apply the standard dual rule.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Here the variable is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the constraint matrix is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, and the cost is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>-coordinate, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> on each slack). The block <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> splits into two families:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>from&#x000A0;the&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;columns</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msup><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">0</mn></mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x021D2;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mtext>from&#x000A0;the&#x000A0;slack&#x000A0;columns</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n  So the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Clean up by flipping signs.</strong> Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> (so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo></mrow></math> turns into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>Reading <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> row by row: for each edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2023A-p4#c": "<p>The LP from part (b) is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Adding the integrality constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> turns this into the <strong>minimum vertex cover</strong> problem.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>What the integral constraint means.</strong> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, read <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> as \"vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is chosen\" and collect <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. The edge constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then says at least one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is chosen — exactly the requirement that the edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is <em>covered</em> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. Imposing this for all edges says <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex cover.</li>\n  <li><strong>What the objective means.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, so we are looking for the smallest set of vertices touching every edge — the minimum vertex cover.</li>\n  <li><strong>The duality picture.</strong> The primal (part (a)) is fractional maximum matching, and its dual (part (b)) is fractional minimum vertex cover. LP duality gives max fractional matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> min fractional vertex cover. The LP solutions may be non-integral in general, which is why we call them \"fractional.\"</li>\n</ol>\n<p>For <em>bipartite</em> graphs the LPs have integral optima on both sides, so the fractional values collapse to genuine matchings and covers: maximum matching <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo></mrow></math> minimum vertex cover. This is König's theorem, obtained here for free from LP duality plus integrality of the bipartite polytope.</p>\n", "2023A-p5#x": "<p>The hint links rank to determinants: a matrix has rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> iff it has some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> submatrix with nonzero determinant. The randomized algorithm exploits this together with Schwartz&ndash;Zippel.</p>\n<div class=\"box\"><strong>Algorithm.</strong> For each starred entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, independently replace it with a uniformly random integer from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; compute the rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math> of the resulting numeric matrix (Gaussian elimination, polynomial time) and return <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>r</mi></mrow></math>.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>The answer we want.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of stars, and for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msub></mrow></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with the stars set to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">R</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>c</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the true optimum, achieved by some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">r</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">a</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">k</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, there exist row set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and column set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> whose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> submatrix has nonzero determinant.</li>\n<li><strong>Set up a polynomial.</strong> Replace the stars by variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The determinant of that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> submatrix is a polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. It is <em>not</em> the zero polynomial, because evaluating it at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> gives the nonzero determinant from step 1.</li>\n<li><strong>Bound the degree.</strong> Each matrix entry is either a constant or a single variable, so it has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; the determinant of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> matrix is a sum of products of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> entries, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Schwartz&ndash;Zippel.</strong> For a nonzero polynomial of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> evaluated at points chosen independently and uniformly from a set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>, the probability of hitting a root is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Correctness &amp; error.</strong> With probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> the random substitution keeps that submatrix determinant nonzero, so the numeric matrix has rank <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>; and it can never exceed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, which is the maximum over all real substitutions. Hence the algorithm returns exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>r</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math> (error <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>). <strong>Running time</strong> is polynomial, since computing a rank via Gaussian elimination is polynomial.</p>", "2023A-p6#x": "<p>We must compute the sumset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>b</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and, for each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, how many pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> produce it — all in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The key insight is that counting sum-representations is exactly polynomial multiplication, which FFT does in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Encode sets as polynomials.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></msub><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. These are \"indicator\" polynomials: the coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise, and similarly for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Read off the product.</strong> In <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Each term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>b</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Interpretation.</strong> Hence the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> counts precisely the number of ways to write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> iff this coefficient is positive, and the coefficient itself is the requested count of representations.</li>\n<li><strong>Complexity.</strong> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> have degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and their product has degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. The FFT-based multiplication algorithm from class multiplies two degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> polynomials in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Algorithm: build <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>f</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, multiply them via FFT in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, then scan the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> — every index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> with a positive coefficient is an element of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>, and its coefficient is the number of representations.</p>\n", "2023B-p1#a": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>, and it is exactly the content of the strong duality theorem for linear programming. Recall the setup: to every primal LP (say a minimization <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> over a polyhedron <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>) there is an associated dual LP (a maximization). We must argue that when the primal polyhedron is bounded and non-empty, the two optimal objective values coincide.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>The primal has a finite optimum.</strong> \"Non-empty\" means the primal is feasible (there is at least one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> in the polyhedron). \"Bounded\" means the objective cannot run off to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math> over the feasible region. A feasible LP whose objective is bounded attains its optimum at a vertex, so the primal optimal value is a finite number.</li>\n  <li><strong>Weak duality gives one inequality.</strong> For every primal-feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> and dual-feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, weak duality gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>(dual&#x000A0;objective)</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>(primal&#x000A0;objective)</mtext></mrow></math>. So the dual is bounded above by the finite primal optimum and cannot be unbounded.</li>\n  <li><strong>Strong duality closes the gap.</strong> The duality theorem states that if the primal is feasible and bounded, then the dual is also feasible, and the two optimal objective values are <em>equal</em>, not merely ordered by weak duality.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The parenthetical remark is the contrapositive intuition: if the dual were infeasible, the primal would be unbounded (or infeasible). Since here the primal is feasible and bounded, the dual must be feasible, and both optima agree.</div>\n", "2023B-p1#b": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. The claim is only about the cost of a <em>single</em> pivoting step, not about how many pivots the whole simplex run performs (that number can be exponential). We check that one pivot is a fixed number of polynomial-size linear-algebra operations.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Choosing the entering variable.</strong> The algorithm computes the reduced-cost vector and picks a coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> whose reduced cost signals that bringing it into the basis improves the objective. Forming the reduced costs is a matrix-vector computation on the input data, hence polynomial in the input length.</li>\n  <li><strong>Choosing the leaving variable.</strong> Given the entering column, the ratio test compares the current basic solution against that column entrywise and selects the coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> to remove from the basis. This is a linear scan of polynomially many ratios.</li>\n  <li><strong>Updating the basis.</strong> Swapping <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> in and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> out requires recomputing the basis inverse and the associated basic solution, i.e. some matrix inversions and matrix multiplications. Each of these runs in polynomial time in the size of the matrices, which are bounded by the input dimensions.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Every ingredient of a pivot — reduced costs, ratio test, and the inverse/multiplication update — is a standard polynomial-time linear-algebra routine, so each pivoting step is polynomial in the input length. The open difficulty of simplex is the possible number of steps, not the cost per step.</div>\n", "2023B-p1#c": "<p>The statement is <strong>false</strong>: no nonzero five-variable polynomial can vanish at every integer point. The Schwartz–Zippel lemma provides an explicit non-root, contradicting the claim.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Set up.</strong> Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a nonzero polynomial and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> be its total degree. We want an integer assignment where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> does not vanish.</li>\n  <li><strong>Pick a finite grid.</strong> Take the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, which has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> integer values. Consider assignments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> drawn from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math>, i.e. each variable ranges over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Apply Schwartz–Zippel.</strong> For a nonzero polynomial of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> and a uniformly random point of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math>, the lemma bounds the probability of hitting a root by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclude a non-root exists.</strong> Since the probability of being a root is strictly less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, some point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>S</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The values <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>5</mn></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> are integers, so we have exhibited an integer assignment on which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> is nonzero. Hence a nonzero polynomial cannot vanish on <em>every</em> integer assignment, and the statement is false.</div>\n", "2023B-p1#d": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>, and it follows from the spectral mixing analysis of random walks on regular graphs seen in class. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the normalized adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>; because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is regular, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is exactly the transition matrix of the random walk, its largest eigenvalue is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with the uniform vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math> as eigenvector, and by assumption every other eigenvalue has absolute value at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Track the distribution.</strong> Start at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>; the initial distribution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is the indicator vector on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math>. After <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps the distribution is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Decompose in the eigenbasis.</strong> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> along the uniform direction plus a remainder orthogonal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>. Applying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> leaves the uniform component fixed (eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) and shrinks every other component by its eigenvalue to the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th power, which is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> in magnitude.</li>\n  <li><strong>Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</strong> Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> is polynomially small, and one gets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> where the error vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> has norm at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The probability of being at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps is the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>-th entry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, namely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"bold-italic\">&#x003B5;</mi><mo fence=\"false\" stretchy=\"false\">&#x02016;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, this entry is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. So for a suitable constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> the walk reaches <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> steps with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</div>\n", "2023B-p2#x": "<p><strong>Statement (weak duality).</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be an LP in standard form, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be its dual, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> free). Then for every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and every feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Use dual feasibility together with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</strong> Dual feasibility says <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, multiplying coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> preserves the direction of the inequality: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Summing over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> gives\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>equivalently</mtext><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><strong>Use primal feasibility <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> into the left side: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Rewrite as scalars.</strong> Both sides are scalars, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, which is the claim <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>&#x0220E;</mo></mrow></math></li>\n</ol>\n<p>The single subtle point is step 1: the inequality <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> may only be multiplied by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup></mrow></math> without flipping because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is nonnegative — this is exactly where primal nonnegativity is used.</p>\n", "2023B-p3#x": "<p>Both standard 2-approximations for vertex cover in ordinary graphs generalize to a 3-approximation on a 3-uniform hypergraph, where each edge is a triple <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and a cover must hit every triple. We give the combinatorial one in full and note the LP version.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Greedy algorithm.</strong> Start with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>. While <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mo>&#x02205;</mo></mrow></math>: pick an arbitrary edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, add all three vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>, and erase every edge that contains at least one of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></math>. Each iteration deletes at least the picked edge, so the loop ends in polynomial time.</li>\n  <li><strong>Output size.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> be the number of iterations. Each adds exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> vertices, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Lower bound on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</strong> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> edges that were picked are pairwise <em>disjoint</em>: when an edge is picked, every other edge sharing a vertex with it is erased, so a later-picked edge shares no vertex with earlier ones. Any valid cover must contain a vertex from each picked edge; since these <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> edges are vertex-disjoint, that forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> <em>distinct</em> vertices. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Ratio.</strong> Combining, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, a 3-approximation.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>C</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>3</mn><mi>k</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>since&#x000A0;the&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>k</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;picked&#x000A0;edges&#x000A0;are&#x000A0;disjoint&#x000A0;and&#x000A0;</mtext><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p><strong>LP-rounding alternative.</strong> The IP is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>w</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Relax to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, solve for an optimal fractional <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, and round <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Feasibility: each edge's three fractional values sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so at least one is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> and gets rounded up, covering the edge. Cost: rounding only raises a variable when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>, so the rounded cost is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>v</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>3</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> — the class analysis with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> in place of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2023B-p4#a": "<p>We must show any Hamiltonian cycle yields a point satisfying every constraint of the integer program. The natural encoding is to let the variables be the indicator of \"is this edge used by the cycle\".</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Given a Hamiltonian cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math>, define <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if the directed edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> belongs to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. These values are in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, satisfying the integrality constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Recall the two families of constraints: for every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, the incoming edges sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>), and the outgoing edges sum to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>w</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>w</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li>A Hamiltonian cycle visits every vertex exactly once and is a single closed tour. Hence at each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> the cycle enters through exactly one edge and leaves through exactly one edge — in-degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and out-degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Therefore, for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, exactly one incoming variable equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (the rest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>), so the incoming sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; likewise exactly one outgoing variable equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the outgoing sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Both constraint families hold.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>All constraints are met, so the edge-indicator vector of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> is a feasible solution to the program. (Its objective value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is exactly the weight of the cycle, which is why the IP models TSP.)</p>\n", "2023B-p4#b": "<p>The converse fails because the degree constraints only enforce \"in-degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and out-degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> at every vertex\" — they say nothing about the selected edges forming a <em>single</em> connected tour. A union of several disjoint cycles satisfies the same degree constraints.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Take a graph on, say, six vertices consisting of two vertex-disjoint directed triangles: cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and cycle <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>4</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>5</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> on these six edges and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> elsewhere.</li>\n<li>Check the constraints: in each triangle every vertex has exactly one incoming and one outgoing chosen edge. So for every vertex the incoming sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and the outgoing sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and all variables are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. This is a feasible IP solution.</li>\n<li>But this edge set is <em>not</em> a Hamiltonian cycle: a Hamiltonian cycle is a single cycle through all vertices, whereas here the vertices split into two separate cycles. The subgraph is not even connected, so it cannot be one cycle covering all six vertices.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">This is the classic \"subtour\" phenomenon: the degree constraints permit any collection of vertex-disjoint cycles that together cover all vertices, not just one grand cycle. That is exactly the gap part (c) fixes with subtour-elimination constraints.</div>\n", "2023B-p4#c": "<p>The new constraints are the <strong>subtour-elimination</strong> constraints: for every proper non-empty subset <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0228A;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math>,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>Q</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mi>:</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>Q</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe double sum counts how many <em>chosen</em> edges have both endpoints inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi></mrow></math>. The claim is that with these added, every feasible IP solution is a single Hamiltonian cycle.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>From the degree constraints alone (parts a–b), any feasible solution is a vertex-disjoint union of directed cycles covering all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> — every vertex has in- and out-degree exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Suppose, for contradiction, this union were more than one cycle. Pick one of the cycles and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> be its set of vertices; since there is another cycle elsewhere, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0228A;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> (a proper subset).</li>\n<li>That cycle uses exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> edges, and all of them stay inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math>. So the internal-edge count for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>But the subtour constraint for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> demands this sum be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>Q</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the constraint is violated — contradiction.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence no feasible solution can contain a cycle on a proper subset of vertices. The only remaining possibility for a union of cycles covering <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> is a single cycle through all vertices, i.e. a Hamiltonian cycle. Together with part (a), the correspondence is now exact.</p>\n", "2023B-p5#x": "<p>The goal is to compute the transitive closure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> — which pairs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are connected by <em>some</em> directed path — in total time asymptotically below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>. The trick is to reduce reachability to a single matrix power, then compute that power with fast matrix multiplication and repeated squaring.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Path counts.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. A standard fact is that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> equals the number of walks of length <em>exactly</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>; in particular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff such a walk exists.</li>\n  <li><strong>Adding self-loops turns \"exactly\" into \"at most\".</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>, the adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> obtained by adding a self-loop at every vertex. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has a path from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> of length <em>at most</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. Reason: expanding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> mixes in the identity, and a self-loop lets us pad any shorter walk out to length exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>; conversely a positive entry corresponds to some walk of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Which power to use.</strong> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> can reach <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> at all, it can do so along a simple path, which uses at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices and hence has length at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. So reachability is captured exactly by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>: set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>E</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>j</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The algorithm.</strong> Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>; compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> by repeated squaring (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02308;</mi><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x02309;</mi></mrow></math> squarings, each a fast matrix multiplication); read off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> from the positive entries.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\nForming <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> and reading off <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>G</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> each cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The power <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> needs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> matrix multiplications, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> total. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>3</mn></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.\n</div>\n<p>Repeated squaring is what keeps the count of multiplications at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> rather than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is obtained by squaring <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> times instead of multiplying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> times.</p>\n", "2023B-p6#x": "<p>The algorithm is the simplest possible: color each element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> red or blue independently and uniformly at random. We show this achieves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> with high probability, for a suitably large constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Encode a color as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</strong> For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is red and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if blue. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent, and for a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> the discrepancy is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>red</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x00023;</mi><mtext>blue</mtext><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Fix one set and bound its discrepancy.</strong> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math> and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Because the target threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, the event of exceeding the larger threshold is contained in that of exceeding the smaller one:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mstyle></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Apply Chernoff/Hoeffding for a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> terms:</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> the exponent is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>t</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>s</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. the bound is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>C</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> large enough makes this at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Union bound over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> sets.</strong>\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">[</mo><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">]</mo></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>So with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> every set has discrepancy below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">D</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">i</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">c</mi></mrow><mi>h</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>C</mi><msqrt><mrow><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msqrt></mrow></math> with high probability.</p>", "2024A-p1#a": "<p>The statement is <strong>false</strong>. It is contradicted directly by the strong duality theorem.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Fix the standard convention: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a minimization LP in standard form, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is its dual, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. \"Optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" is the minimized value, \"optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" is the maximized value.</li>\n  <li>Weak duality already gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> for all feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>opt</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mtext>opt</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> always. The statement asks whether this can be a <em>strict</em> inequality when both are feasible.</li>\n  <li>Strong duality says: if both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are feasible, then both optima are finite and <em>equal</em>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>opt</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>opt</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. There is no duality gap.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Because the two optimal values must coincide, it is impossible for the optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to be strictly greater than the optimum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence the claimed situation cannot occur, and the statement is false.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#b": "<p>The statement is <strong>false</strong>. A nonnegative determinant is far weaker than positive semidefiniteness, and a single counterexample settles it.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>2pt</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mn>0</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>. It is symmetric (equal to its transpose), so it qualifies as a candidate for the statement.</li>\n  <li>Its determinant is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the hypothesis \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" holds.</li>\n  <li>But <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is not PSD. Recall <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is PSD iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, equivalently iff all its eigenvalues are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> has eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> with multiplicity two, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo>&#x02016;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is negative definite, the opposite of PSD.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<p>Why the implication fails in general: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is only the <em>product</em> of the eigenvalues. An even number of negative eigenvalues (here two) makes the product positive while the matrix is nowhere near PSD. The determinant sees the parity of the negative eigenvalues, not whether any exist.</p>\n</div>\n", "2024A-p1#c": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>: the Gram–Schmidt process constructs an orthonormal basis, and every operation it performs is a polynomial-time vector computation.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>First reduce to independent inputs. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> are linearly dependent, greedily keep a maximal linearly independent subset (add vectors one at a time, dropping any that lies in the span of those already kept — an independence test is a rank computation, done in polynomial time). The kept vectors span the same subspace, so assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent.</li>\n  <li>Build an orthogonal basis by removing, from each new vector, its components along the ones already produced. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> and, for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>,\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>These <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are pairwise orthogonal. Indeed, for any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, the coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is chosen precisely so that\n  <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mi>&#x027E8;</mi><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub><mi>&#x027E9;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\n  (using that earlier <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>&#x02113;</mi></msub></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02113;</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> are already orthogonal to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>).</li>\n  <li>Finally normalize each vector, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>&#x02016;</mo><msub><mover><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02016;</mo></mrow></math>, producing an orthonormal basis.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Each step uses finitely many inner products and vector subtractions on vectors in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, and there are at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps, so the whole procedure runs in polynomial time.</p>\n", "2024A-p1#d": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. Strictly positive reduced costs certify that the basic feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> beats every other feasible point, uniquely.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Setup: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. At the basic feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> with basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> (nonbasic set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>) we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>, so the objective value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Express any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) through the basis. From <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>, hence\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n  where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> is exactly the reduced-cost vector.</li>\n  <li>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and every entry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> is strictly positive, the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is optimal.</li>\n  <li>Uniqueness: if any coordinate of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math> were positive, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is not optimal. Thus any optimum must have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> is the one and only optimal solution.</p>\n", "2024A-p2#x": "<p>We want to estimate the density <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — the fraction of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> assignments that satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math> — to within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, with failure probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The idea is Monte-Carlo sampling: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is exactly the probability that a uniformly random assignment satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>, so estimate that probability by sampling.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Algorithm.</strong> Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> independent uniformly random assignments <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, evaluate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> for each (checking a CNF against an assignment is fast — linear in the formula size), and output the fraction that satisfy: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Unbiasedness.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math>\". Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is uniform, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, linearity gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — our estimator is centered on the right value.</li>\n<li><strong>Concentration.</strong> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent and bounded in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. The Chernoff/Hoeffding bound gives, for the <em>bad</em> event,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C6;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n(The printed line's <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> direction is a typo; the quantity being bounded is the deviation exceeding <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>.)</li>\n<li><strong>Choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> large enough that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Then the failure probability is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, i.e. success <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> samples, each evaluated in polynomial time, the whole algorithm runs in polynomial time, as required.</p>\n", "2024A-p3#x": "<p>We must find a polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> so that random edge weights from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> make the minimum-weight path unique for <em>every</em> pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> simultaneously, with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>. The plan is: apply the Isolation Lemma to a single fixed pair to bound the chance its optimum is not unique, then take a union bound over all pairs.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Recall the Isolation Lemma.</strong> Given a universe of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> elements, each assigned an independent uniformly random integer weight in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and any family of subsets of the universe, the probability that the minimum-weight subset in the family is <em>not</em> unique is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Set up one pair.</strong> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> and let the family <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> paths, each identified with its set of edges; the universe is the edge set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>. The number of edges is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math> as the weight range <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Bound a \"bad\" pair.</strong> Call <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> <em>bad</em> if the minimum-weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math>–<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> path is not unique. By the lemma the probability of this is at most\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><strong>Union bound over all pairs.</strong> There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> pairs. The probability that <em>some</em> pair is bad is at most\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\nWith <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>10</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></math>, the probability that any pair fails is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>; hence with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> every pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> has a unique minimum-weight path.\n</div>\n<p>The same random weights are used for all pairs at once, which is why the single union bound over the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> pairs suffices.</p>\n", "2024A-p4#x": "<p>Weighted Max-SAT: given a CNF formula <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C6;</mi></mrow></math> with variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> and clauses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math> of nonnegative weights <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math>, find a truth assignment maximizing the total weight of satisfied clauses. We encode it as an integer program with two families of binary variables.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Variables: for each Boolean variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is set true. For each clause <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, intended to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> (resp. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>) be the set of variables appearing positively (resp. negated) in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</div>\n<p>The IP is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>maximize</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>A literal-satisfaction count.</strong> For a positive literal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff that literal is true; for a negative literal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000AC;</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is false, i.e. iff that literal is true. So the right-hand side counts how many literals of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> are satisfied.</li>\n<li><strong>The constraint links <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> to satisfaction.</strong> If no literal of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied, the sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, forcing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. If at least one literal is satisfied, the sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is allowed to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> has positive coefficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> in a maximization, the optimum sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> whenever it is permitted. Thus at optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is satisfied.</li>\n<li><strong>Feasible solutions match assignments.</strong> Any assignment to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the same as choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>; the constraints then permit <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly for satisfied clauses, and the objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the total satisfied weight. Maximizing it therefore solves weighted Max-SAT.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024A-p6#x": "<p>We must decide whether the subspace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>span</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> contains an invertible matrix, i.e. whether some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invertible. The idea is to test invertibility at a random point and use the Schwartz–Zippel lemma.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Algorithm.</strong> Draw <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; answer \"yes\" iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invertible (checked in polynomial time via a determinant / Gaussian elimination).</li>\n  <li><strong>Key polynomial.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>k</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo></mrow></math>. Each entry of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a linear form in the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and a determinant of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix is a degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> polynomial in its entries; hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> has total degree at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. A matrix is invertible iff its determinant is nonzero, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invertible exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>No false \"yes\".</strong> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> contains no invertible matrix then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the tested matrix is always singular, and we correctly answer \"no\".</li>\n  <li><strong>Rare false \"no\".</strong> If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> does contain an invertible matrix, there is an assignment with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is a nonzero polynomial of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. By Schwartz–Zippel, for random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B1;</mi></mrow></math> drawn from a set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>,\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>P</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>So when an invertible matrix exists the algorithm says \"yes\" with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>9</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>10</mn></mrow></math>, and it never errs otherwise: a one-sided-error, polynomial-time randomized decider. (The success probability can be boosted by independent repetitions.)</p>\n", "2024B-p1#a": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. Solving integer linear programs (ILP) is NP-hard, because many NP-complete problems encode naturally as ILPs. So a polynomial-time ILP solver would place an NP-hard problem in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00050;</mi></mrow></math>, collapsing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00050;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Recall the reduction seen in class from <strong>maximum independent set</strong>. Given a graph <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and a target <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, introduce a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math> for each vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>, with the integrality/bound constraints <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For every edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>E</mi></mrow></math> add the constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>u</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>; this forbids selecting both endpoints, so the chosen vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> form an independent set.</li>\n<li>Maximize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>v</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The optimum equals the size of the maximum independent set, and it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> has an independent set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>. This transformation is clearly polynomial.</li>\n<li>Since deciding maximum independent set is NP-complete, a polynomial-time ILP algorithm would solve it (and every NP problem, via such encodings) in polynomial time.</li>\n<li>ILP is itself in NP (a satisfying integer assignment is a short certificate). An NP-hard problem solvable in polynomial time forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00050;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">N</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024B-p1#b": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. For an LP in standard form the feasible region is the polytope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, and there is a clean algebraic test for a point being a vertex, all of whose steps run in polynomial time.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Feasibility.</strong> First check that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> even lies in the polytope: verify <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> (a matrix-vector product and comparison) and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (coordinate-wise). Both are trivially polynomial. If either fails, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is not a vertex.</li>\n<li><strong>Vertex characterization from class.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the submatrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> consisting of exactly those columns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (the support of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>). The result proved in class is: a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex if and only if these columns are linearly independent, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> has full column rank.</li>\n<li><strong>Checking the rank.</strong> Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>rank</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> via Gaussian elimination, which is polynomial time, and compare it to the number of positive coordinates (the number of columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math>). Equality means full column rank.</li>\n<li>Combining: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex exactly when it is feasible and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> has full column rank—both verifiable in polynomial time, so the decision algorithm is efficient.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024B-p1#d": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. The quantity in question, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mstyle scriptlevel=\"1\"><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable></mstyle></mrow></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mstyle></mrow></math>, is by definition the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th elementary symmetric polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: the sum, over all size-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> subsets, of the products of those eigenvalues. The key is that these symmetric functions of the eigenvalues appear directly as coefficients of the characteristic polynomial, which we can compute efficiently even though we cannot find the eigenvalues themselves.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>The characteristic polynomial of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> factors over its eigenvalues as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Expanding this product, the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. This is exactly the relationship between roots and coefficients (Vieta's formulas).</li>\n<li>Taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>: the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>coefficient&#x000A0;of&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mtext>&#x000A0;in&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>p</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>In class we saw an algorithm that computes <em>all</em> coefficients of the characteristic polynomial of a given matrix in polynomial time (without computing eigenvalues). Read off the coefficient of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> and multiply by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>This yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>, hence the desired sum, efficiently.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024B-p2#x": "<p>We compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> with a divide-and-conquer recursion, exactly like FFT, exploiting the block structure of the Walsh–Hadamard matrix\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Split the input.</strong> Write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is the top half and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> the bottom half, each of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Use the block formula.</strong> Multiplying out the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> block matrix by the stacked halves,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe crucial point: only <em>two distinct</em> smaller products appear, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math>. We do not recompute them for each block.</li>\n<li><strong>Recurse then combine.</strong> Recursively compute the two half-size products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub></mrow></math> (each on a vector of length <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>). Then form the top half by adding them and the bottom half by subtracting them; this combining is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> additions and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> subtractions, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> work.</li>\n<li><strong>Base case.</strong> At <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>1</mn></mtd><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>H</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is computed directly in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p><strong>Running time.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the cost on length-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> input. We make two recursive calls on size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> plus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> combining, so\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nBy the master theorem (the classic FFT/merge-sort recurrence), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>N</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, as required.</p>\n", "2024B-p3#x": "<p>The bound falls out of Karger's success-probability analysis together with the observation that the algorithm returns at most one cut, so different cuts correspond to disjoint events.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Single-cut guarantee.</strong> Fix any particular minimum cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. As shown in class, one run of Karger's contraction algorithm outputs exactly this cut with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> (the probability that no edge crossing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is ever contracted). This lower bound holds for <em>every</em> minimum cut, since the analysis used nothing special about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> beyond it being minimum.</li>\n  <li><strong>Disjoint events.</strong> On any single run the algorithm outputs one specific cut, so the events \"the algorithm returns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" for distinct cuts <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> are mutually exclusive. Therefore the probabilities of returning the various minimum cuts add up, and their sum is at most the probability of returning some minimum cut, which is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Sum and rearrange.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be the set of minimum cuts. Combining the two points,\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>the&#x000A0;algorithm&#x000A0;returns&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<p>Solving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mi>M</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, the claimed bound on the number of minimum cuts.</p>\n", "2024B-p4#x": "<p>We compute the diameter <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>max</mo><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></msub><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of a strongly connected digraph in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> using fast matrix multiplication (exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>) and binary search over the diameter value. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the adjacency matrix.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Reachability via powers.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> counts walks of length exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff there is a walk of length <em>at most</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> — the added identity lets a walk \"stay put\", so reaching in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> steps is captured. Equivalently <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Finite, bounded diameter.</strong> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is strongly connected every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is finite, and a shortest path never repeats a vertex, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>; the diameter is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Binary search.</strong> The diameter is the smallest <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> for which <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for <em>all</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Test a candidate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>: if all entries of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> are positive the diameter is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, otherwise it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>; recurse on the appropriate half of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. This is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> tests.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cost.</strong> Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mi>k</mi></msup></mrow></math> (with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>) is computed by repeated squaring in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and scanning all entries for positivity is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, negligible. Total <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<p>Sharper: precompute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>B</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>i</mi></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> once, in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> total. Then each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> binary-search steps only multiplies by an already-stored power, so the whole search also runs in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n</div>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, as required.</p>\n", "2024B-p5#x": "<p>We want a minimum-weight <em>representative</em> set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>V</mi></mrow></math> — one meeting every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> — where each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>. This is a hitting-set problem, and we <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-approximate it by relaxing and rounding the given integer program <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Relax and solve.</strong> Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and solve the LP in polynomial time; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> be an optimal fractional solution, with value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Round.</strong> For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, else <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> be the set whose characteristic vector is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> is representative.</strong> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. The constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is a sum of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> terms, so their average is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>; hence some <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>H</mi></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> hits every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>T</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Weight bound.</strong> For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> this holds as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then by construction <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mi>w</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>H</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>d</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>The first inequality multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>d</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and sums; the last uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>, which holds because every integer solution is LP-feasible, so relaxing only lowers the minimum. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>H</mi></mrow></math> is a representative set of weight at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> times optimal — a polynomial-time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n", "2024B-p6#x": "<p>We must produce, in poly time, a bipartite graph that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-balanced with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>. \"Balanced\" means: for every size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>T</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, the number of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mtext>–</mtext><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> edges is close to its \"ideal\" value: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: put each of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> possible edges independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math> is a sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> independent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> variables with mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>; Chernoff makes each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> deviate with tiny probability, and a union bound survives all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> pairs once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is large enough.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Algorithm & expectation.</strong> For every pair <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>R</mi></mrow></math>, independently add edge <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is an edge, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. For fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>T</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, a sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> independent indicators, so by linearity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Chernoff for one pair.</strong> The deviation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> is a constant fraction of the mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, so the Chernoff bound gives an exponentially small tail: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for an absolute constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (the failure of the balance condition for that single <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li><strong>Union bound over pairs of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> The number of ways to pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00028;</mo><mfrac linethickness=\"0\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo minsize=\"2.047em\" maxsize=\"2.047em\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>T</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;of&#x000A0;size&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>m</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;that&#x000A0;fail</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Choosing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, the exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>m</mi><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>m</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> becomes very negative once <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> beats <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Taking <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> large enough forces this bound below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Union bound over sizes.</strong> Sum the failure probability over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> (at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> values): total failure <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Hence with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>10</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> the graph is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-balanced. Generating the graph takes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> coin flips, i.e. poly time.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2024C-p1#a": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. It follows from the class characterization of vertices of a standard-form polytope <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>x</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> columns.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Recall the theorem proved in class: a feasible point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex (a basic feasible solution) if and only if the columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> indexed by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>supp</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> are linearly independent — equivalently, the submatrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi></msub></mrow></math> of those columns has full column rank.</li>\n  <li>This associates to every vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> a specific subset of columns, namely its support. The map \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>supp</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>\" is <em>injective</em>: if two vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> had the same support <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, then both would satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. Since the columns in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> are linearly independent, that system has at most one solution, forcing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>Therefore distinct vertices give distinct subsets of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. The total number of subsets of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-element set is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>An injection from the set of vertices into the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> column-subsets shows the number of vertices is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>. (The bound is loose — most subsets are either not independent or do not solve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>z</mi><mi>S</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> — but as an upper bound it is correct.)</p>\n", "2024C-p1#b": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>, and it is exactly what the Schwartz–Zippel lemma buys us: a nonzero low-degree polynomial cannot vanish on a large enough grid of integer points.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> be a nonzero polynomial of total degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0211A;</mi></mrow></math>. Draw each argument <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from the finite set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, which has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> elements.</li>\n  <li>Schwartz–Zippel bounds the chance of accidentally hitting a root:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mspace width=\"0.278em\" /><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. So the probability that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> vanishes is strictly below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, which means the complementary event <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has strictly positive probability.</li>\n  <li>An event of positive probability over a finite sample space cannot be empty: there must exist at least one concrete choice <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The probabilistic language is just a counting device — it certifies that a nonzero point exists inside the grid <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, which is precisely the claim.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#c": "<p>Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> denotes the class of languages <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> that have a randomized algorithm which accepts inputs outside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>, and accepts inputs inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. The two classes look similar but the gap between the thresholds is everything, so the statement is <strong>false</strong>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the two thresholds coincide: on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02209;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> we need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>accept</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, and on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> we need <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>accept</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The trivial algorithm that flips one fair coin and accepts on heads has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>accept</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> on <em>every</em> input, so it satisfies both conditions simultaneously. Hence <em>every</em> language <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> — even an undecidable one such as the halting problem — lies in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li>For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> there is a genuine gap. Given a halting randomized algorithm <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>, we can decide <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>L</mi></mrow></math> deterministically: enumerate all possible coin-toss strings (finitely many, since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi></mrow></math> halts and uses boundedly many random bits), compute the <em>exact</em> acceptance probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math>, and answer \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>L</mi></mrow></math>\" iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>. Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>p</mi></mrow></math> is guaranteed to be either <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, this always decides correctly and halts. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains only decidable languages.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains undecidable languages while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">B</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> contains none, the classes cannot be equal.</p>\n", "2024C-p1#d": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. The key is that the number of perfect matchings of a bipartite graph is the <em>permanent</em> of its biadjacency matrix, and the permanent equals the determinant when we work modulo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>Let the two sides of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vertices each, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if left-vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is adjacent to right-vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. A perfect matching is a bijection <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> pairing left <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> with right <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> using only real edges, i.e. one with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. The product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> exactly when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi></mrow></math> is such a matching and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise, so the number of perfect matchings is\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>perm</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>The determinant is the same sum with signs:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>sgn</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><msubsup><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C3;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li>Modulo <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> the sign disappears, because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so every term of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> equals the corresponding term of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>perm</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mo>perm</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">m</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">o</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">d</mi></mrow><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>2</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff the number of perfect matchings is odd.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The determinant is computable in polynomial time (e.g. Gaussian elimination), so the algorithm just computes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and outputs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>mod</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. This decides the parity efficiently.</p>\n", "2024C-p2#x": "<p>Boolean product is defined by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02218;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x022C1;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02227;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: entry <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff some index <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> has both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Computing this directly costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. We beat that by reducing to <em>ordinary integer</em> matrix multiplication, which has fast <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> algorithms with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: a Boolean OR-of-ANDs is positive exactly when the corresponding integer inner product is positive. So run fast matrix multiplication over the integers, then threshold each entry at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Rewrite AND/OR as arithmetic.</strong> Treat the 0/1 entries as integers. For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02227;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> equals the integer product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> (it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> iff both are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>). The OR over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> is true iff at least one product is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. iff the integer sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>B</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is strictly positive.</li>\n<li><strong>Equivalence.</strong> Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02218;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is the standard integer matrix product. (The sum is a nonnegative integer; it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> iff every term is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.)</li>\n<li><strong>Compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> fast.</strong> Run a fast matrix-multiplication algorithm over the integers in time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (e.g. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.37</mn></mrow></math>). This dominates the runtime.</li>\n<li><strong>Threshold.</strong> Output the matrix whose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> entry is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>C</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Scanning all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> entries costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Total time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, as required.</p>\n", "2024C-p3#x": "<p>We want a single vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> far from <em>every</em> vector in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, specifically <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The algorithm is as simple as possible: <strong>pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> uniformly at random</strong>, i.e. each coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. We show it works with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Expected distance to one fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Fix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is fixed and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>u</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a fair coin, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. The Hamming distance is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, so by linearity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Concentration for one fixed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a sum of independent bits and Chernoff applies. The bad event <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> means <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> is below its mean by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Chernoff gives\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math></div>\nfor some constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (the deviation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>6</mn></mrow></math> is a constant fraction of the mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, giving exponential decay).</li>\n<li><strong>Union bound over all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> The vector fails only if it is too close to <em>some</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>. Summing the per-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> failure probability,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02203;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>This is tiny for large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn><mi>ln</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> grows only polynomially (logarithmically in the exponent) while <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math> grows exponentially, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>2024</mn></mrow></msup><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; in particular it is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math> for large enough <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.9</mn></mrow></math> the random <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>u</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></math>, as required.</p>\n", "2024C-p4#x": "<p>Since no bin can help beyond one-item-per-bin, at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> bins are ever needed; so index candidate bins <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Use two families of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variables.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Variables.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: does bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> get used (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>) or not (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>: is item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> placed in bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Objective.</strong> Minimize the number of used bins: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Each item placed exactly once:</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> values, this forces exactly one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math> to be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. item <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> sits in exactly one bin.</li>\n<li><strong>Capacity, tied to \"used\":</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>B</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> for every bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. The left side is the total weight loaded into bin <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. If <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> it reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> (respect capacity); if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> it reads <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, which (as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>w</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) forces every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> — nothing may be placed in an unused bin. This single constraint enforces both capacity and the meaning of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Why every feasible IP solution is a valid packing: the placement constraints put each item in exactly one bin; the capacity constraints keep each used bin within weight <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>; and any bin holding an item is forced to have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> truly counts the bins used. Conversely any real packing yields a feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> of equal cost, so the IP optimum equals the minimum number of bins.</div>\n", "2024C-p6#x": "<p>We are given <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and its dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Assume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is infeasible and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has a feasible point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>; we show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is unbounded by exhibiting feasible dual points whose objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Get an infeasibility certificate from Farkas' Lemma.</strong> The system <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is infeasible, so by (this form of) Farkas' Lemma there exists a vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> with\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>and</mtext><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> is the direction we will move along.</li>\n<li><strong>Build a family of dual points.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> define <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is the given feasible dual point.</li>\n<li><strong>Check <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub></mrow></math> stays feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</strong> Compute\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nNow <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> is feasible, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> (that is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> transposed) with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Adding, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>: feasible for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Show the objective blows up.</strong> The dual objective at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub></mrow></math> is\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nSince <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x02032;</mi></msup></mrow></math> is positive and grows without bound as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>&#x003BB;</mi></msub><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo>&#x0221E;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>We have produced feasible dual solutions with arbitrarily large objective value, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is unbounded, as claimed.</p>", "2025A-p1#a": "<p>The claim is <strong>true</strong>. Read the statement carefully: it asks whether there is <em>always</em> an assignment satisfying <em>at most</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses. This is a weaker demand than the familiar \"at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>\" statement, and it follows from the same averaging (probabilistic-method) argument.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Pick a uniformly random assignment: set each variable to true or false independently with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>. A fixed clause of a 3-CNF has 3 literals; it is <em>unsatisfied</em> only when all 3 literals are false, which happens with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>. So it is satisfied with probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi></mrow></math> be the number of satisfied clauses. By linearity of expectation, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>clause&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>j</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;satisfied</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>8</mn></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>A random variable cannot always lie strictly above its mean, nor always strictly below it. Formally, if every assignment satisfied strictly more than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>Y</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>, a contradiction. Hence there exists at least one assignment with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>That is exactly the requested assignment satisfying at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math> clauses, so the statement holds. Note the same expectation also gives an assignment satisfying <em>at least</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>7</mn><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>8</mn></mrow></math>; the mean is sandwiched, so both directions have witnesses.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#b": "<p>The claim is <strong>true</strong>. We compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> by <em>repeated squaring</em>, and each polynomial multiplication is done with FFT, which multiplies two degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> polynomials in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is a power of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>, write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: square <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> times. Starting from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>1</mn></msup></mrow></math>, squaring gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mn>8</mn></msup></mrow></math>, up to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>. That is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> multiplications, not <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Track the degree. The polynomial before the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th squaring is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>f</mi><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></msup></mrow></math>, which has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; we multiply it by itself. So the operands of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>-th squaring have degree on the order of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. One FFT multiplication of degree-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>D</mi></mrow></math> polynomials costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, giving cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mrow></math> for step <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Sum over all squarings: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>O</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Simplify the logarithm. Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> throughout, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>log</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Pull that factor out:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><mi>O</mi><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"prefix\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" form=\"postfix\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li>The geometric sum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Therefore the total is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>So the coefficients of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> are computed in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> arithmetic operations, matching the stated bound. The whole cost is dominated by the final squaring, whose operands have degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x00398;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#c": "<p>The claim is <strong>false</strong>. The statement asserts that computing the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> <em>requires</em> time <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>; the word \"requires\" means it is a proven lower bound. It is not.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Stack the vectors as the columns of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. Then the matrix product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> has columns <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>. So computing all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> products is the same task as one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix multiplication.</li>\n<li>Fast matrix multiplication (Strassen and its successors) multiplies two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> time, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> (Strassen already gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>log</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mn>7</mn><mo>&#x02248;</mo><mn>2.81</mn></mrow></math>). This beats the naive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>The hypothesis that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> are linearly independent is irrelevant: the algorithm forms <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> and multiplies regardless of any relationship among the columns, so it never uses independence.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Since there is an algorithm running in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, the claim that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup></mrow></math> time is required is refuted.</p>\n", "2025A-p1#d": "<p>The claim is <strong>true</strong>. The set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msup><mi>&#x0211D;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> is precisely the eigenspace of the adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> for eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so its dimension equals the multiplicity of the eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. We must argue this multiplicity is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for a connected regular graph.</p>\n<p>A subtlety: the statement uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> (the adjacency matrix) and eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. For a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math>-regular graph the all-ones vector satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>, so eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> here refers to the <em>normalized</em> setting where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is divided by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi></mrow></math> (the random-walk / normalized adjacency matrix), whose top eigenvalue is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Under that normalization the argument is exactly the one seen in class and the homework.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>The all-ones vector is an eigenvector with eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: each row of the normalized matrix sums to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mrow><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></mrow></math>. Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is indeed an eigenvalue and the eigenspace has dimension at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Regular graphs are symmetric, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> is diagonalizable with real eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, and all eigenvalues satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>It was proved in the homework that if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is <em>connected</em> then the second eigenvalue is strictly smaller than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. This is the spectral characterization of connectivity: a spectral gap at the top.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Since only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> equals <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the multiplicity of the eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, so the eigenspace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> has dimension exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025A-p2#a": "<p>To dualize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the solution first rewrites <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> in the equality \"standard form\" that the dual recipe applies to, then reads off the dual, and finally cleans it up with a sign change. The one place symmetry is used is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Standard form.</strong> Flip each \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math>\" constraint by multiplying by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>&#x027FA;</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. Then absorb the slack with non-negative variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> to make it an equality: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. This gives\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>x</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><strong>Apply the dual recipe.</strong> For a standard-form primal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>b</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>M</mi><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msup><mi>d</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>free</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003BE;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>d</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math> splits into two blocks: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; the objective is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>. Using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>:\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><strong>Tidy up.</strong> Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> (so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math>):\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mo>max</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>Notice <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> has the same data <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, just with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>min</mo></mrow></math> swapped for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo>max</mo></mrow></math>: thanks to symmetry the problem is essentially self-dual. This is exactly what part (b) exploits.</p>\n", "2025A-p2#b": "<p>We are given a point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and must show it is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. The idea is that this single point is simultaneously feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and for its dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> from part (a), and it gives the two problems the <em>same</em> objective value — which, by duality, forces it to be optimal.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> requires <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, in particular <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>; and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> is given. Both hold.</li>\n  <li><strong><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is feasible for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</strong> Recall <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>:</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>max</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><mi>z</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>A</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>: from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Both hold, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is dual-feasible too.</li>\n  <li><strong>Equal objective values.</strong> The primal objective at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; the dual objective at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. So this one point achieves the identical value in both problems.</li>\n  <li><strong>Conclude optimality.</strong> Weak duality says every primal-feasible value is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02265;</mo></mrow></math> every dual-feasible value (<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>max</mo></mrow></math>). Here a primal-feasible point and a dual-feasible point attain the <em>same</em> value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mrow><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></mrow></msup><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. No primal solution can go below it (it would fall under a feasible dual value, contradicting weak duality), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> achieves the primal minimum. Hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> (and, symmetrically, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>).</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\nA point that is feasible for both a primal and its dual with equal objective values is optimal for both. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> makes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math> such a point, it is optimal for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.\n</div>\n", "2025A-p3#x": "<p>We must model Minimum Makespan — assign <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> jobs with processing times <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>p</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> parallel machines so that the most heavily loaded machine finishes as early as possible — as an integer linear program. The trick for turning a \"minimize the maximum load\" objective into a linear one is to introduce an auxiliary variable that upper-bounds every load.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Assignment variables.</strong> For each job <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> and machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> take a boolean variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> means job <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> is placed on machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Makespan variable.</strong> Introduce a variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> meant to represent the makespan (the finishing time of the last machine), which we will minimize.</li>\n  <li><strong>The program.</strong> <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mrow><mtext>minimize</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&</mi><mi>T</mi><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mi>&</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>T</mi><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace linebreak=\"newline\" /><mi>&</mi><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>&</mi><mi>&</mi><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The first constraints force each job onto exactly one machine (a valid assignment). The second constraints say the load <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>p</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> of every machine <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> is an upper bound on all loads; minimizing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> drives it down to the maximum load itself. Hence the optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>T</mi></mrow></math> equals the minimum achievable makespan.</div>\n", "2025A-p4#x": "<p>The algorithm is pure random sampling: pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> and output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>s</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. We show this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>-unbiased with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.99</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Each character is a fair coin.</strong> Fix a nonempty <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. For a uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math>, the value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x0220F;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>I</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a product of independent uniform <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> variables, hence itself uniform on <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>; in particular it has mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a sum of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> independent, mean-zero, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x000B1;</mi><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>-bounded variables.</li>\n  <li><strong>Chernoff for one <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Because the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent, a Chernoff/Hoeffding bound gives, for a constant <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>,\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>s</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>s</mi><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mstyle></mrow></math></div></li>\n  <li><strong>Choose the sample size.</strong> Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> large enough that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>; solving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow></math> needs only <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>ln</mi><mn>2</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>c</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, which is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Union bound over all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> There are fewer than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> nonempty subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math>. By the union bound the probability that <em>some</em> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>v</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>s</mi></mrow></math> is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Thus with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0.99</mn></mrow></math>, every nonempty <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>S</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>e</mi><mi>I</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math>-unbiased. Sampling <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> bits, the algorithm runs in time polynomial in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n", "2025A-p5#x": "<p>Two directed graphs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> are determinantally equivalent if the determinants of their symbolic adjacency matrices are identical <em>as polynomials</em> in the variables <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. We cannot expand these determinants symbolically (they can have exponentially many monomials), so we test the polynomial identity by evaluating at a random point—this is exactly polynomial identity testing via Schwartz–Zippel.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Algorithm.</strong> For every variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, pick an independent uniformly random integer <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>. Evaluate both determinants numerically (each is just the determinant of a concrete number matrix, computable in polynomial time). Declare <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>G</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></mrow></math> determinantally equivalent iff the two numbers are equal.</li>\n<li><strong>Setup for correctness.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be the symbolic adjacency matrix of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>G</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and define the polynomial <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. Every determinant of an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrix is a sum of products of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> entries, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Equivalent case (no false negatives).</strong> If the graphs are equivalent then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi></mrow></math> evaluates to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> for <em>any</em> choice of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>r</mi><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub></mrow></math>, the two determinants agree, and the algorithm correctly says \"equivalent\" with certainty.</li>\n<li><strong>Non-equivalent case (bounded false positives).</strong> If they are not equivalent then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>P</mi><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. By the Schwartz–Zippel lemma, a nonzero polynomial of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> evaluated at a random point over a set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> vanishes with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>P</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>100</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>So the algorithm errs only in the non-equivalent case, with probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>100</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>. It runs in polynomial time (two determinant evaluations), meeting the requirement.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025B-p1#a": "<p>The statement is <strong>false</strong>. The claim is that some nonzero polynomial of degree less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> vanishes at <em>every</em> point whose coordinates are order-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity. Schwartz–Zippel rules this out.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mi>&#x02102;</mi></mrow></math> be the set of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>-th roots of unity. There are exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> of them, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Suppose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is a nonzero polynomial with total degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Draw each coordinate <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math> independently and uniformly from the set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>The Schwartz–Zippel lemma bounds the chance a nonzero polynomial vanishes on a random point drawn from a finite set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi></mrow></math> per coordinate:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mrow><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nwhere the last inequality is exactly the hypothesis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Since the probability of vanishing is strictly less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, the event \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>\" has positive probability. A positive-probability event is non-empty, so there <em>exists</em> a point <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msubsup><mi>&#x003A9;</mi><mi>m</mi><mrow><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>n</mi></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>a</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Therefore no nonzero polynomial of degree below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> can vanish on all such points, contradicting the claim. The probabilistic argument is just a clean way to prove existence of a non-root.</p>\n", "2025B-p1#b": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. The trick is to reduce the rectangular product to several square products and then invoke fast matrix multiplication.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> be <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Cut <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> vertically into <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> square blocks, each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Matrix multiplication distributes over this block structure column-block by column-block: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>. So computing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> reduces to computing the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> square products <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>n</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is a product of two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> matrices, which fast matrix multiplication does in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> operations, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> is the matrix-multiplication exponent (known to be roughly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>2.37</mn></mrow></math>).</li>\n<li>There are <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> such products, so the total cost is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math>, we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>4</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mn>4</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math> for large <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. Note this genuinely requires fast multiplication: the naive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> per block would give <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>, which is <em>not</em> strictly less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>. It is exactly the sub-cubic exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> that pushes us below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>4</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</div>\n", "2025B-p1#c": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>, and it is a direct application of Markov's inequality. The key move is to turn a statement about students into a statement about a single random variable.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Pick a student uniformly at random and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> be that student's grade. Because the student is uniform, for any threshold <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math> the probability <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> equals exactly the <em>fraction</em> of students whose grade is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>. This is what lets us translate \"at most half the students\" into \"probability at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>2</mn></mrow></math>\".</li>\n<li>The average grade of the class is precisely <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, the mean of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow></math> over the uniform choice of student.</li>\n<li>Grades are non-negative, so Markov's inequality applies: for a non-negative random variable and any <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mi>a</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>a</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Apply it with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>a</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math> (twice the average):\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mn>2</mn><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li>By Step 1, the left-hand side is the fraction of students scoring at least twice the average. Hence at most half the class can achieve this.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>(This implicitly assumes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x1D53C;</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. the average is not zero; otherwise \"twice the average\" is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and the statement is trivial anyway. Markov gives the bound for free precisely because it needs nothing beyond non-negativity.)</p>\n", "2025B-p1#d": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. The solution connects a determinant condition to an eigenvalue, and then invokes a spectral characterization of bipartiteness proved in class.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>A determinant is zero exactly when the matrix is singular, i.e. has a non-trivial kernel. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> means there is a <em>nonzero</em> vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Expand: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>v</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>v</mi></mrow></math>. By definition this says <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>v</mi></mrow></math> is an eigenvector of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> with eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. So <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is an eigenvalue of the normalized adjacency matrix <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Recall the spectral facts about the normalized adjacency matrix of a graph: all its eigenvalues lie in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, the largest is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (with multiplicity one when the graph is connected), and the smallest possible value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is attained <em>if and only if</em> the graph is bipartite. This is the theorem proved in class.</li>\n<li>Since we just showed <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is an eigenvalue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>, the \"only if\" direction of that theorem forces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> to be bipartite.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The chain is: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">det</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is an eigenvalue of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x021D2;</mo></mrow></math> (by the class theorem) <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is bipartite. Connectivity is used implicitly so that the spectral characterization applies cleanly.</div>\n", "2025B-p2#a": "<p>In weighted set cover the input is subsets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo>&#x02286;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> with costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math>, every element lying in at least one set, and we want a minimum-cost subfamily whose union is all of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. We model the yes/no choice \"is set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> taken?\" with a binary variable.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li>For each set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> introduce <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> meaning <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is in the cover and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> meaning it is not.</li>\n  <li>The cost of the chosen family is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>m</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; since we want the cheapest cover, the objective is to minimize it.</li>\n  <li>\"Covering\" means every element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is contained in at least one chosen set. The sets containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> are those <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, and the number of chosen such sets is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Requiring this to be at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> gives one constraint per element: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"1em\" /><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mspace width=\"1em\" /><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>Each element-constraint guarantees at least one chosen set covers that element, so any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector is exactly (the indicator of) a valid cover, and its objective is that cover's total cost. An optimal integer solution is therefore a minimum-weight set cover. Feasibility is never empty because each element already appears in some set.</p>\n", "2025B-p2#b": "<p>Frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> means every element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> lies in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> of the sets. We use the LP-relaxation-and-round paradigm on the program from (a) to get a cover of cost at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Relax.</strong> Replace <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and solve the resulting LP in polynomial time; call the optimum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>x</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Its value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>, because every integer cover is also LP-feasible, so relaxing can only lower the minimum.</li>\n  <li><strong>Round.</strong> Include set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> in the cover iff <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>; let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> be this <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> vector.</li>\n  <li><strong>Feasibility.</strong> Fix an element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Its constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is a sum of at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> terms (frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>). If all were below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> the sum would be below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>f</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, a contradiction; so some set containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> has <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and is taken. Thus every element is covered.</li>\n  <li><strong>Cost.</strong> For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math>: if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> this holds since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>; if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> then by the rounding rule <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mtext>ALG</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>f</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n</div>\n<p>The first inequality multiplies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msubsup><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi><mrow><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></msubsup></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and sums; the last step uses <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>LPOPT</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>OPT</mtext></mrow></math>. So this polynomial-time algorithm returns a valid cover of cost at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> times optimal, an <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation.</p>\n", "2025B-p3#x": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> be a basic feasible solution of a standard-form LP (minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>) with basis <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>B</mi></mrow></math>; split the columns of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> into the basic part <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> and the nonbasic part <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and correspondingly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. We show that if the reduced cost vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is optimal. The idea is to rewrite the objective so its dependence on the (free) nonbasic variables is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Express basic variables.</strong> The equality constraint is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math> is invertible (it is a basis), <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>. This holds for <em>every</em> feasible point, not just <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Substitute into the objective.</strong> Then\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\nThe bracketed row vector is precisely the reduced cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Value at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> At the basic feasible solution <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> the nonbasic variables vanish, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Lower bound for any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> The identity in step 2 applies to any feasible <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, giving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the term <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msubsup><mover><mi>c</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007E;</mo></mover><mi>N</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>N</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><msubsup><mi>c</mi><mi>B</mi><mi>T</mi></msubsup><msubsup><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msubsup><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mi>T</mi></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> attains the smallest objective value among all feasible points, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is optimal.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025B-p4#x": "<p>We are told <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>deg</mi><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> at all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th roots of unity, where <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> is a primitive <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th root of unity. We recover <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> by working in the \"point-value\" domain: evaluate, divide pointwise, then interpolate.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Evaluate.</strong> Evaluate both <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi></mrow></math> at the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. Evaluating a polynomial of degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> at all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th roots of unity is exactly a DFT, computable by the FFT in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>Divide pointwise.</strong> For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> compute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. These <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> divisions cost <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and they are well-defined because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> by assumption, so no division by zero occurs.</li>\n  <li><strong>Interpolate.</strong> Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>, we have <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, hence <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>g</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>h</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>i</mi></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> exactly. So the quotients from Step 2 are the values of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math> at the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity. Interpolating a polynomial from its values at the roots of unity is an inverse DFT, again <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> via the FFT.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Correctness of the output: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>h</mi><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, and a polynomial of degree less than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> is uniquely determined by its values at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> distinct points; the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> roots of unity are distinct, so the interpolated polynomial is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>h</mi></mrow></math>. Total running time is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</div>\n", "2025B-p5#x": "<p>We estimate the versatility <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, the fraction of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msup></mrow></math> colorings that are legal, by sampling colorings and reporting the empirical legal-fraction. The only tools needed are a legality check and a Chernoff (Hoeffding) bound.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>The algorithm.</strong> Pick <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> independent, uniformly random colorings <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>t</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><mi>V</mi><mo>&#x02192;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> (choose each vertex's color uniformly). For each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, check legality by scanning all edges and verifying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>u</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02260;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>v</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>; this takes linear time. Output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>t</mi></mfrac><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></msubsup><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>, the fraction that are legal.</li>\n  <li><strong>Expectation.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> be an indicator random variable for the event that <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> is legal. A uniform coloring is legal with probability equal to (number of legal colorings)<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mn>3</mn><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. With <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> we get <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>t</mi><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and the output <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>X</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> has mean <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> — it is an unbiased estimator.</li>\n  <li><strong>Concentration.</strong> The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>X</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> are independent and lie in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so the additive Chernoff/Hoeffding bound gives <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mi>A</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mspace width=\"negativethinmathspace\" /><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">[</mo><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi>X</mi></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"double-struck\">E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>X</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.623em\" maxsize=\"1.623em\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div> The exponent <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> comes from the deviation <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> entering the bound as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mi>t</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, so the failure probability is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>e</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></msup><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>. The algorithm samples <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>3</mn></msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> colorings and checks each in linear time, so it runs in polynomial time and outputs an estimate within <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>R</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>G</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn>2</mn><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</div>\n", "2025C-p1#a": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. Here \"root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>\" means any complex number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th root of unity, not necessarily primitive). We are told <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> and must show every such <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> also satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> divides <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>, we can write <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> for some natural number <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> be any root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>, so by definition <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mn>1</mn><mi>m</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, using the exponent law <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mrow><mi>k</mi><mi>m</mi></mrow></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>k</mi></msup><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>m</mi></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Thus <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>&#x003C9;</mi><mi>n</mi></msup><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> is a root of unity of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>. As <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003C9;</mi></mrow></math> was arbitrary, every order-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> root of unity is an order-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> root of unity.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Geometrically, the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math>-th roots of unity are the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> evenly spaced points on the unit circle, and when <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> they form a subset of the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math> evenly spaced <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi></mrow></math>-th roots of unity.</div>\n", "2025C-p1#b": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> denotes the minimum number of scalar multiplications needed to multiply two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> matrices. We must exhibit <em>some</em> algorithm for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> matrices using <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> scalar multiplications; since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub></mrow></math> is the minimum over all algorithms, this proves <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> be two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math> matrices. Partition each into a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> grid of blocks, where every block is itself a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> matrix. This is consistent because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Matrix multiplication respects this block structure: the product <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>B</mi></mrow></math> is computed by the same formulas as a <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> matrix product, except the \"entries\" are the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> blocks and \"entry multiplication\" is block (matrix) multiplication.</li>\n<li>By definition of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math>, multiplying these <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> arrays of blocks can be done with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> block-multiplications (the additions of blocks are free, as they use no scalar multiplications).</li>\n<li>Each such block-multiplication is a product of two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>k</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>k</mi></mrow></math> matrices, which again by definition costs <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub></mrow></math> scalar multiplications.</li>\n<li>Hence the total number of scalar multiplications is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>. Since this is a valid method, the minimum satisfies <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mrow><msup><mi>k</mi><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003B1;</mi><mi>k</mi></msub><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mn>2</mn></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p1#c": "<p>The statement is <strong>true</strong>, and it is a direct application of the isolation lemma. Recall the lemma: for any family <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> of subsets of a ground set of size <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, if each element gets an independent uniform weight from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> and set-weight is the sum of element weights, then the probability that the minimum-weight set in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> is <em>not</em> unique is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li>Take the ground set to be the edge set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>E</mi></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>, and let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x02131;</mi></mrow></math> be the family of edge-subsets that are exactly the cuts of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math>. The weight of a cut is the sum of weights of its crossing edges—precisely the sum form the lemma needs.</li>\n<li>Weights are drawn uniformly and independently from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></math>. The lemma gives <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>minimum&#x000A0;cut&#x000A0;not&#x000A0;unique</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mi>N</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>4</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>E</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>For the maximum cut, apply the same argument to the negated weights (maximizing a sum is minimizing its negation; the value set is still <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>N</mi></mrow></math> distinct values). This yields <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mtext>maximum&#x000A0;cut&#x000A0;not&#x000A0;unique</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> as well.</li>\n<li>By a union bound, the probability that <em>at least one</em> of the two failure events occurs is at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>4</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li>Therefore, with probability at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>2</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, both the minimum-weight cut and the maximum-weight cut are unique.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p1#d": "<p>The statement is <strong>false</strong>. The claimed equivalence \"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x027FA;</mi></mrow></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> is a vertex\" fails in <em>both</em> directions, and a single simple example already breaks it.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>An optimal solution need not be a vertex.</strong> Consider the program: minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> subject to <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. The objective is constant, so <em>every</em> feasible point is optimal—including all the non-vertex points in the open interval <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, not just the vertices <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Thus optimality does not force being a vertex.</li>\n<li><strong>A vertex need not be optimal.</strong> In the same box <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, if instead we minimize <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi></mrow></math>, the vertex <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> is feasible but has value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, while the optimum is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> at the other vertex. So a vertex can be strictly suboptimal.</li>\n<li>What <em>is</em> true (and what the false statement distorts) is a one-directional fact: if the LP is feasible and bounded, then there <em>exists</em> an optimal solution that is a vertex. That guarantees an optimal vertex, but says nothing forcing every optimum to be a vertex or every vertex to be optimal.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p2#a": "<p>We must show <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> is the LP dual of the set-cover relaxation. The relaxation replaces <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>i</mi><mi>:</mi><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0220B;</mo><mi>j</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> for every element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>. The clean way is to write it in matrix standard (equality) form and use the known dual rule.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Matrix form.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>n</mi><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> incidence matrix with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>A</mi><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>i</mi></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> if <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> otherwise. Then constraint <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>. With cost vector <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> the relaxation is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>To equality standard form.</strong> Rewrite <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> and add nonnegative slacks <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>. Stacking variables, this is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>x</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>s</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>x</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mi>s</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, and the objective is still <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>x</mi></mrow></math> (slacks have zero cost).</li>\n<li><strong>Apply the standard-form dual rule.</strong> For <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>min</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>z</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>z</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><msup><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover></mrow></math>, with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> free. Here <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>M</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x02223;</mo><mi>I</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>b</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mover accent=\"true\"><mi>c</mi><mo>&#x0005E;</mo></mover><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>c</mi><mi>;</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>. So the dual is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>max</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> s.t. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mi>I</mi></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x0005B;</mo><mtable><mtr><mtd><mi>c</mi></mtd></mtr><mtr><mtd><mn>0</mn></mtd></mtr></mtable><mo>&#x0005D;</mo></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Substitute <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x021A6;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>y</mi></mrow></math></strong> (so the new <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>). Then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mn mathvariant=\"bold\">1</mn><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>, and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math> becomes <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>A</mi><mo>&#x022A4;</mo></msup><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>c</mi></mrow></math>, i.e. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> for each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi></mrow></math>. This is exactly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">Dual <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"true\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mo>max</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>s.t.</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02200;</mo><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mspace width=\"2em\" /><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mstyle></mrow></math></div>", "2025C-p2#b": "<p>Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math> be an optimal solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, and let the chosen family be the sets whose dual constraint is <em>tight</em>: <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mi>:</mi><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math>. We prove <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math> covers every element. The argument is by contradiction, exploiting optimality of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Assume not a cover.</strong> Suppose some element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is covered by no set in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math>. Then every set <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> that contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> is <em>not</em> in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math>, so its constraint is slack:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mspace width=\"2em\" /><mtext>for&#x000A0;every&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;with&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>The variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> has room to grow.</strong> The dual variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> appears only in the constraints of sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> &mdash; and we just saw all of those are strict inequalities. Constraints of sets not containing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></math> do not involve <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> at all, so raising <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> leaves them untouched.</li>\n<li><strong>Push <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub></mrow></math> up.</strong> Choose <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> small enough that each strict slack <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> can absorb <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> (take <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> below the smallest such positive slack). Setting <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>y</mi><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x02190;</mo><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>j</mi><mn>0</mn></msub></mrow></msub><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mi>&#x003B5;</mi></mrow></math> keeps <em>all</em> constraints (2) satisfied and keeps <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>y</mi><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, so the new point is feasible.</li>\n<li><strong>Contradiction.</strong> The objective <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msub><mi>y</mi><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> increases by <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x003B5;</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, giving a feasible dual solution strictly better than <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math>. This contradicts the optimality of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>Hence no element can be left uncovered, so the family of tight sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"script\">F</mi></mrow></math> is a set cover.</p>", "2025C-p2#c": "<p>The algorithm: solve the dual LP <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> to get an optimal <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><mo>&#x0002A;</mo></mrow></math>, then output the family <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> of sets whose dual constraint is tight (by part (b) this is a valid set cover). Solving an LP and reading off tight constraints is polynomial time, so the algorithm is efficient. We bound its cost using the frequency-<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> assumption: every element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> lies in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> of the sets <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>m</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Tightness rewrites each set's cost.</strong> For every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> the constraint is tight, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mi>:</mi><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math>. Summing over the chosen sets,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Swap the order of summation.</strong> Group the double sum by element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>: each <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is counted once for every chosen set that contains <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math>. Writing <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo></mrow></math> for that count,\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></msub><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Apply the frequency bound.</strong> Element <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>j</mi></mrow></math> appears in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets overall, hence in at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math> sets of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math>. Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, each coefficient is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>:\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi><mi>:</mi><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x02208;</mo><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"true\" fence=\"true\" minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">|</mo><mspace width=\"0.167em\" /><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mtext>&#x000A0;</mtext><mi>f</mi><msubsup><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><mi>j</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow><mi>n</mi></msubsup><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n<li><strong>Relate to the optimum.</strong> The sum <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub><msup><mi>y</mi><mi>\\</mi></msup><msub><mo>&#x0002A;</mo><mi>j</mi></msub></mrow></math> is the optimal value of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>D</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, which equals the optimal value <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math> of the primal relaxation (LP duality). Since the relaxation lower-bounds the integral set cover, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>. Therefore\n<div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><msub><mo>&#x02211;</mo><mrow><msub><mi>S</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mi>C</mi></mrow></msub><msub><mi>c</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">L</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div></li>\n</ol>\n<div class=\"box\">The returned cover costs at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi><mo>&#x000B7;</mo><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">T</mi></mrow></mrow></math>, so this is an efficient <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>-approximation for set cover with frequency <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>f</mi></mrow></math>.</div>", "2025C-p3#x": "<p>The solution reduces the lazy walk to the standard random-walk theorem from class by expressing its transition matrix through the graph's normalized adjacency matrix and checking that all its eigenvalues lie in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, with the second-largest magnitude bounded away from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>. Once those spectral facts hold, \"the rest is identical to class.\"</p>\n<div class=\"box\">Key idea: laziness <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> only rescales eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>; this keeps the top eigenvalue at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and pushes all others safely inside <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002F;</mo><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so the class mixing argument applies verbatim.</div>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n<li><strong>Class facts about <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>.</strong> Let <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> be the normalized adjacency matrix (a step to a uniform random neighbor). Since <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>G</mi></mrow></math> is regular and connected, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>'s eigenvalues satisfy <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, with the spectral-gap bound <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Transition matrix of the lazy walk.</strong> Each step stays with prob. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> term) or moves to a uniform neighbor with prob. <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> (the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> term), so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>I</mi><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mi>A</mi></mrow></math>. Because <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>I</mi></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>A</mi></mrow></math> share eigenvectors, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>C</mi></mrow></math> has the same eigenvectors with eigenvalues <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>Top eigenvalue unchanged:</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (the stationary/uniform direction survives).</li>\n<li><strong>Second eigenvalue bounded below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>:</strong> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">(</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>4</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><mo minsize=\"1.2em\" maxsize=\"1.2em\">)</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. So there is still a spectral gap of order <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msup><mi>n</mi><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>5</mn></mrow></msup></mrow></math>.</li>\n<li><strong>No negative eigenvalue hurts.</strong> The point of laziness: from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math>, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002B;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><msub><mi>&#x003BC;</mi><mi>n</mi></msub><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>2</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>. Thus every <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo>&#x02208;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mn>3</mn></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo></mrow></math>, so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mi>i</mi></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><msub><mi>&#x003BB;</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math> for all <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>i</mi><mo>&#x0003E;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> — no eigenvalue near <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> can slow mixing.</li>\n<li><strong>Conclusion.</strong> With top eigenvalue <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> and all others of magnitude <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>, the class theorem gives: after <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mtext>poly</mtext><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math> steps (roughly <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>O</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup><mi>log</mi><mi>n</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>, enough for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>6</mn><msup><mi>n</mi><mn>5</mn></msup></mrow></mfrac></mstyle><msup><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mi>t</mi></msup></mrow></math> to become small) the distribution is close to uniform, so the probability of being at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>t</mi></mrow></math> is at least <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo>&#x02212;</mo><mi>o</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mi>n</mi></mfrac></mstyle><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mo>&#x02265;</mo><mstyle displaystyle=\"false\" scriptlevel=\"0\"><mfrac><mn>1</mn><mrow><mn>2</mn><mi>n</mi></mrow></mfrac></mstyle></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n", "2025C-p4#x": "<p>Each branching program computes the <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-entry of a product of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> matrices whose entries are variables or rational scalars. Two programs compute the same polynomial iff the difference of these two polynomials is identically zero, which we test by random evaluation via Schwartz–Zippel.</p>\n<ol class=\"gsteps\">\n  <li><strong>Degree bound.</strong> Every entry of a single matrix has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> (it is a variable or a constant). When two matrices are multiplied, each entry of the product is a sum of products of two entries, so degrees add by at most <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow></math> per multiplication. Multiplying <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> matrices therefore yields entries of total degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>. Hence the polynomials <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>A</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> and <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mn>1</mn></msub><mo>&#x022EF;</mo><msub><mi>B</mi><mi>m</mi></msub><msub><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo><mrow><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msub></mrow></math> each have degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and so does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>A</mi></msub><mo>&#x02212;</mo><msub><mi>P</mi><mi>B</mi></msub></mrow></math>.</li>\n  <li><strong>The test.</strong> Assign each variable <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><msub><mi>x</mi><mi>i</mi></msub></mrow></math> an independent uniform value from <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>S</mi><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007B;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mo>&#x02026;</mo><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>m</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007D;</mo></mrow></math> (so <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mn>100</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>), compute both matrix products numerically, and declare \"equal\" iff the two <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00028;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>&#x0002C;</mo><mn>1</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x00029;</mo></mrow></math>-entries agree.</li>\n  <li><strong>Error analysis.</strong> If the programs compute the same polynomial then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02261;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math>, the entries always match, and the test is correct with certainty. If they differ then <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>Q</mi><mo>&#x02262;</mo><mn>0</mn></mrow></math> has degree <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mi>m</mi></mrow></math>, and by Schwartz–Zippel\n  <div class=\"gmath\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"block\"><mrow><mo movablelimits=\"true\">Pr</mo><mo stretchy=\"false\">[</mo><mi>Q</mi><mtext>&#x000A0;evaluates&#x000A0;to&#x000A0;</mtext><mn>0</mn><mo stretchy=\"false\">]</mo><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>deg</mi><mi>Q</mi></mrow><mrow><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo><mi>S</mi><mo stretchy=\"false\">&#x0007C;</mo></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x02264;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn><mi>m</mi></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003D;</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>1</mn></mrow><mrow><mn>100</mn></mrow></mfrac><mo>&#x0003C;</mo><mn>0.1</mn><mo>&#x0002E;</mo></mrow></math></div>\n  So a false \"equal\" verdict occurs with probability under <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math>.</li>\n</ol>\n<p>The error is one-sided and below <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>0.1</mn></mrow></math> as required. Efficiency: each run does <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>m</mi></mrow></math> multiplications of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mn>3</mn><mo>&#x000D7;</mo><mn>3</mn></mrow></math> matrices over <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\" display=\"inline\"><mrow><mi>&#x0211A;</mi></mrow></math> with numbers of polynomially bounded size, so it runs in polynomial time.</p>\n"};
